/* ---------- guided lessons ---------- */
function lessonGo(cfg){
  if(cfg.level!=null)loadLevel(cfg.level);   // always start from the level's defaults
  for(const[k,v]of Object.entries(cfg.set||{}))setParam(k,v,false);
  if(cfg.stepKind)AN.stepKind=cfg.stepKind;if(cfg.stepAmp!=null)AN.stepAmp=cfg.stepAmp;if(cfg.injAmp)AN.injAmp=cfg.injAmp;
  AN.adv=true;AN.wi=null;AN.loop=cfg.loop||'lat';AN.curveR=cfg.curveR||0;AN.useEst=!!cfg.useEst;AN.meas={step:null,freq:null,spd:null};
  syncAnControls();renderAllPanes();setMode('an');
  if(cfg.test==='step'){runStepTest();renderAnalysis();}else if(cfg.test==='inj')runInjection();else if(cfg.test==='spdstep'){if(!AN.resSpd)spdCompute();runSpeedStepTest();renderAnalysis();}
}
const LV_DIFF=0,LV_CAR=5,LV_MOOSE=6;   // levels that set up each vehicle; L7 the moose test
const LESSONS=[
 {t:'The loop being analyzed',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Every tracker in this lab, and every lane-keeping controller on a real vehicle, closes the same loop: measure how far the vehicle is from the path, steer, and the steering changes the error. Everything about how that loop behaves, how fast it corrects, how much it overshoots, how much delay it tolerates, follows from one small linear model. Learning to read that model is what turns tuning from trial and error into engineering, and it is what the Analyze view computes live for whatever controller and vehicle you have set.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Near a straight path at a steady speed, with small errors, the vehicle is two integrators in series: the steering command sets the rate of the heading error, and the heading error sets the rate of the lateral error. The lateral error is therefore the double integral of what the controller commands. A controller that fed back only the lateral error would oscillate for ever (a double integrator with proportional feedback is an undamped spring), so every tracker also feeds back the heading error, and since on a straight path the heading error is the lateral error\'s rate of change divided by speed, that second term is derivative action. The whole family of trackers, pure pursuit, rear-wheel feedback, Stanley, PID, even MPC near the path, reduces to two gains on these two errors.</p>
<div class="eq">θ̇ₑ = g·u      u = ω, g = 1 (diff-drive)     u = δ, g = v₀/L (car)
ė  = v₀·θₑ
u  = −K_e·e − K_θ·θₑ     every tracker near the path, each with its own K_e and K_θ</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The Frenet equations ė = <i>v</i> sin θ<sub>e</sub>, θ̇<sub>e</sub> = ω − <i>v</i>κ cos θ<sub>e</sub>/(1 − κ<i>e</i>) hold for any vehicle whose velocity points along its heading; on a straight path (κ = 0) with small θ<sub>e</sub> they are the two lines above. For the car, ω = <i>v</i> tan δ/<i>L</i> ≈ (<i>v</i>/<i>L</i>)δ gives the plant gain <i>g</i>. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/1-the-vehicle-in-path-coordinates">Derivation: chapter 6 §1</a> · the Frenet equations and the trackers\' gains: <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch5/1-path-coordinates-and-the-speed-profile">chapter 5 §1</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The analysis does not trust formulas for the gains; it reads them from the controller\'s own code at the operating point by finite differences, nudging <i>e</i> and θ<sub>e</sub> by a millimetre and a milliradian and recording the change in the command. It then adds what the formulas leave out: the 25 ms hold delay of the 50 ms tick (a sampled controller holds each command for a tick, which on average delays it by half of one), any control latency, and actuator lag. The chips at the top of the Analyze view summarize the result: crossover, phase margin, gain margin, delay margin, <i>M</i><sub>s</sub>, the dominant poles. Everything updates as you move sliders in the Control and Vehicle tabs; <b>A</b> switches between the analysis and the map.</p>
<p><b>The chips, one line each.</b> You will meet all of them properly in the next lessons; for now, what each one measures. <i>Crossover</i> ω<sub>c</sub>: the frequency where the loop\'s gain is 1, which is how fast the loop corrects (a higher crossover is a faster loop). <i>Phase margin</i>: how far the loop is from oscillating at crossover, in degrees; above about 45° is comfortable, below 30° rings. <i>Gain margin</i>: how much the gain could grow before the loop goes unstable, in dB. <i>Delay margin</i>: the extra delay, in seconds, the loop can take before it goes unstable; it is the phase margin divided by the crossover. <i>M</i><sub>s</sub>: the worst amplification of a disturbance anywhere in frequency; near 1 is calm, above 2 is nervous. <i>Dominant poles</i>: the slowest way the error dies out, read as a damping ratio ζ (0.7 is a gentle return, 0.3 overshoots) and a natural frequency. Lessons 3 to 6 take them one at a time.</p>`,
  tries:[['Open the analysis (diff-drive, pure pursuit)',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp'}},
    {setup:'L1 defaults: the diff-drive robot at 0.8 m/s with pure pursuit (lookahead 0.5 + 0.5·v = 0.9 m). Opens the Analyze view on the lateral loop.',
     see:'Gains read from the code: K_e = 1.98, K_θ = 1.78 (chapter 5 §2 predicts 2v/L_d² = 1.98 and 2v/L_d = 1.78). Crossover 1.95 rad/s, phase margin 62.7°, gain margin 31 dB, delay margin 0.56 s, M_s = 1.05, dominant poles with ζ = 0.71 and 4.3 % predicted overshoot. The Bode plot shows the −40 dB/decade slope of the two integrators bending to −20 at the controller\'s zero.',
     shows:'This is a comfortable loop: half a second of delay budget, almost no disturbance amplification, critically-damped-ish poles. Every number here will move in the lessons that follow, and the first thing to learn is which slider moves which: the lookahead sets the crossover, the vehicle and its delays set what the margins cost.',
     predict:{q:'How much extra delay will this loop absorb before it goes unstable?',opts:['About one 50 ms tick','About half a second','Several seconds'],a:1}}],
   ['Same loop on the car',{level:LV_CAR,set:{ctl:'pp'}},
    {setup:'L6, the car at 0.6 m/s with pure pursuit; the steering angle is the command and the plant gain is v₀/L = 1.0.',
     see:'Nearly the same picture: crossover 2.27 rad/s, phase margin 62.3°, delay margin 0.48 s, ζ = 0.71. The steady error to a bias is different, 0.28 against 0.51 per unit of bias, because the car\'s command is an angle and the diff-drive\'s a rate.',
     shows:'The loop does not care much which vehicle closes it, as long as the model is the two integrators. What the vehicle changes is the plant gain (speed over wheelbase for the car), the actuator (an angle with a rate limit instead of a turn rate), and, in chapter 1, where the model stops being true: at speeds where the tyres, not the geometry, decide the turn.',
     predict:{q:'Switching from the diff-drive to the car with the same pure pursuit: what happens to crossover and damping?',opts:['Both change a lot: the car is a different plant','Damping drops because the command is an angle, not a rate','Roughly the same crossover and the same ζ ≈ 0.71'],a:2}}]],
  q:[['Why does feedback on e alone oscillate for ever?','Two integrators with a proportional gain give ë = −K_e v e: a mass on a spring with no damper. The solution is a sinusoid that never decays. Feedback on θ_e = ė/v is the damper. Every tracker has one; pure pursuit hides it in the geometry of the carrot.'],
     ['The hold delay is half a tick, 25 ms. Why half and not a whole tick?','A zero-order hold applies a command for the whole tick, so the command is on average 25 ms old while it acts; in the frequency domain that is a phase lag of ωT/2, which is what the analysis uses. The full tick shows up elsewhere: the controller sees a measurement that is already up to one tick old, which the latency slider adds on top.'],
     ['The gains are read by finite differences rather than from formulas. What does that buy?','It works for every controller the same way, including MPC, Stanley\'s arctangent and a custom controller from the Code tab, and it reads the gains the code actually has, saturations and all, at the actual operating point, on a curve included. The formulas in the chapters are for understanding; the chips are for truth.']],
  check:{q:'On a straight path at v₀ = 10 m/s the heading error is θ_e = 2° (use the small-angle model ė = v₀·θ_e with θ_e in radians). How fast is the lateral error growing, in m/s?',a:0.349,tol:0.01,unit:'m/s',how:'θ_e = 2° = 0.0349 rad; ė = v₀·θ_e = 10 × 0.0349 = 0.349 m/s.'}},
 {t:'Pure pursuit is a PD controller',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Pure pursuit is the most used path tracker in mobile robotics because it has one parameter and a geometric picture anyone can draw. What the picture hides is that it is a PD controller, proportional-derivative, with a fixed damping ratio, and that its one parameter, the lookahead, sets the bandwidth of the loop and nothing else. Once that is visible, every pure-pursuit tuning rule ("lengthen the lookahead if it oscillates") becomes a statement about crossover frequency and delay, and the limits of the method become predictable.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> The bearing to a carrot <i>L</i><sub>d</sub> ahead on the path is, for small errors, α ≈ −<i>e</i>/<i>L</i><sub>d</sub> − θ<sub>e</sub>: the lateral error seen from a distance <i>L</i><sub>d</sub>, plus the heading error. Pure pursuit commands the curvature 2 sin α/<i>L</i><sub>d</sub>, so linearized it is feedback on both errors with gains fixed by geometry, and the ratio of the two gains is fixed too. Substituting into the two-integrator plant gives a second-order loop whose damping ratio is 1/√2 for any lookahead and any speed. Without delay the phase margin would be 65.5° always; what the lookahead changes is the crossover, 2.2 <i>v</i>₀/<i>L</i><sub>d</sub>, and what delay then costs is ω<sub>c</sub>·<i>T</i><sub>d</sub> radians of that margin, so a short lookahead, a fast loop, loses more to the same delay.</p>
<div class="eq">ω = −(2v₀/L_d²)·e − (2v₀/L_d)·θₑ
L(s) = (2v₀²/L_d²) · (1 + s·L_d/v₀) / s² · e^(−sT_d)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The circle tangent to the heading through the carrot has curvature 2 sin α/<i>L</i> by the inscribed-angle theorem; the linearization of α gives the gains; solving |<i>L</i>(jω)| = 1 for the undelayed loop gives ω<sub>c</sub> = 2.2 <i>v</i>₀/<i>L</i><sub>d</sub> and the phase at crossover, 180° − 65.5°. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch5/2-pure-pursuit">Geometry and gains: chapter 5 §2</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/2-pure-pursuit-is-a-pd-controller-in-disguise">the loop and its crossover: chapter 6 §2</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The lookahead is <i>L</i><sub>d</sub> = <i>L</i>₀ + <i>k</i>·<i>v</i>, two sliders in the Control tab; the buttons here set <i>k</i> = 0 so that <i>L</i>₀ alone is the lookahead. The car\'s δ ≈ 2<i>L</i>α/<i>L</i><sub>d</sub> with <i>g</i> = <i>v</i>₀/<i>L</i> gives the same loop. The step test shifts the path sideways by 0.2 m in the full simulator and overlays the measured response on the linear prediction.</p>`,
  tries:[['L<sub>0</sub> = 1.5 m (slow, calm)',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:1.5,ppK:0}},
    {setup:'Lookahead fixed at 1.5 m, diff-drive at 0.8 m/s.',
     see:'Crossover 1.17 rad/s (2.2 × 0.8/1.5 = 1.17), phase margin 63.9°, delay margin 0.95 s, M_s = 1.03, ζ = 0.71. The Bode magnitude crosses 0 dB low and the phase is still far from −180° there.',
     shows:'A long lookahead is a slow loop: it would take about 4 s to settle a step, and it corrects errors gently, cutting corners on a curvy path. In exchange it tolerates nearly a second of extra delay. The damping is the same 0.71 it always is.',
     predict:{q:'Lengthening the lookahead to 1.5 m: what happens to the crossover and the delay margin?',opts:['Crossover falls; delay margin grows to nearly a second','Crossover rises; delay margin shrinks','Crossover unchanged; only the damping changes'],a:0}}],
   ['L<sub>0</sub> = 0.1 m (fast)',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.1,ppK:0}},
    {setup:'Lookahead fixed at 0.1 m: the carrot is almost under the robot.',
     see:'Crossover 17.6 rad/s, fifteen times higher, and the delay has eaten the margin: phase margin 40.4°, gain margin 11 dB, delay margin 0.04 s, M_s = 1.65. The predicted overshoot is still only 4.6 % because the dominant poles still have ζ ≈ 0.7; what has changed is how close the loop is to its limit.',
     shows:'The 25 ms hold delay costs ω_c·T_d = 17.6 × 0.025 rad = 25° of phase at this crossover, against 1.7° in the slow loop (1.17 × 0.025 rad). The same controller, the same vehicle, the same damping by design, and a loop that any further 40 ms of delay would push unstable. Lookahead is not a comfort setting; it is the loop\'s bandwidth, and bandwidth is paid for in delay tolerance.',
     predict:{q:'With the carrot 0.1 m ahead, what will the phase margin and the damping do?',opts:['PM stays near 63° and ζ 0.71: lookahead only sets speed','PM drops to about 40°, ζ still near 0.7','The loop goes unstable from the hold delay alone'],a:1}}],
   ['Fast loop, step test',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.1,ppK:0},test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.2},
    {setup:'The step test on the fast loop (0.1 m lookahead): a 0.2 m sideways shift of the path, run in the full simulator.',
     see:'The dashed amber measured response lands on the linear prediction: 2 % overshoot measured against 4.6 % predicted, rise 0.55 s, settled in 1.2 s. The two curves differ only where the turn-rate limit briefly clips the first command.',
     shows:'The linear model is a good model of the simulator for small signals, which is the whole justification for analyzing a nonlinear robot with Bode plots. The small-signal limit is tested in the linear-versus-nonlinear lesson; here, 0.2 m is small.',
     predict:{q:'The 0.2 m step on the fast loop in the full simulator: how will the measured response compare with the prediction?',opts:['It rings: the simulator has delays the model lacks','Much slower: the turn-rate limit dominates','It lands on the prediction, with a few per cent overshoot'],a:2}}]],
  q:[['Pure pursuit has ζ = 0.71 regardless of settings. Where does that number come from?','From the ratio of the two geometric gains: K_θ/K_e = L_d, so ζ = K_θ/(2√(K_e v)) = (2v/L_d)/(2√(2v²/L_d²)) = 1/√2. The lookahead cancels. Rear-wheel feedback has two gains and so two knobs; pure pursuit has one and spends it on bandwidth.'],
     ['Why does the undelayed phase margin come out as exactly 65.5°?','Because L(jω) = K(1 + jωL_d/v)/(jω)² has phase −180° + atan(ωL_d/v), and at the crossover of this particular loop ωL_d/v = 2.2 for every setting, giving atan(2.2) = 65.5°. It is a property of the fixed gain ratio, not of the vehicle.'],
     ['A real vehicle at 15 m/s with a 10 m lookahead: what crossover and what delay budget?','ω_c ≈ 2.2 × 15/10 = 3.3 rad/s. With the undelayed margin of 65.5° and a typical 100 ms of sensing-to-actuation latency costing 0.33 rad = 19°, about 46° remain, a delay margin near 0.25 s. The same arithmetic as the fast loop here, at a different scale.']],
  check:{q:'Pure pursuit at v₀ = 12 m/s with a lookahead L_d = 8 m: what is the crossover frequency ω_c ≈ 2.2 v₀/L_d, in rad/s?',a:3.3,tol:0.07,unit:'rad/s',how:'ω_c = 2.2 × 12/8 = 3.3 rad/s.'}},
 {t:'Reading the Bode plot',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> The Bode plot is the control engineer\'s X-ray: two curves that show, frequency by frequency, how much the open loop amplifies and how much it delays, and from which the stability margins can be read by eye. The lessons that follow refer to it constantly. This one is about reading it: what the slopes mean, where the margins are, and what the closed-loop curves add.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> The top panel is |<i>L</i>(jω)| in decibels against frequency on a log axis, the bottom its phase. Two integrators give a −40 dB/decade slope at −180° of phase: a sinusoidal steering input at any frequency comes out as a lateral error exactly out of phase and shrinking with the square of frequency. The controller\'s zero at s = −K_e·v₀/K_θ bends the slope to −20 dB/decade above that frequency and lifts the phase toward −90°, which is where the margin comes from. Delay subtracts phase in proportion to frequency, ω<i>T</i><sub>d</sub>, without touching the magnitude. The crossover ω_c, where |L| = 0 dB, is roughly the closed-loop bandwidth; the phase margin is how far above −180° the phase is there; the gain margin is how far below 0 dB the magnitude is where the phase reaches −180°. A rule of thumb for second-order loops: ζ ≈ PM/100 below about 70°.</p>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> |<i>L</i>| and arg <i>L</i> are the magnitude and angle of the complex number <i>L</i>(jω), and the closed loop 1/(1 + <i>L</i>) blows up where <i>L</i> = −1, which is 0 dB and −180° on this plot; the margins measure the distance from that point along each axis. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/3-the-open-loop-l-s-with-the-clock-in-it">The open loop with the clock in it: chapter 6 §3</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/4-reading-the-margins">reading the margins: §4</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> With "+ closed loop" on, two more curves appear: green is |T| = |L/(1 + L)|, how a path offset at each frequency is followed, and pink is |S| = |1/(1 + L)|, how a disturbance at each frequency is amplified or suppressed; the peak of |<i>S</i>| is <i>M</i><sub>s</sub>, one number that covers both margins. The dotted line at π/T = 62.8 rad/s is the Nyquist frequency of the 20 Hz loop: nothing above it exists in a sampled controller. The injection experiment adds a small sine at the actuator at nine frequencies and plots the measured |<i>L</i>| and phase as points on the model curves.</p>`,
  tries:[['Rear-wheel feedback, k_e = 2',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'rwf',rwKe:2,rwKth:3}},
    {setup:'Rear-wheel feedback with a soft lateral gain, k_e = 2, and k_θ = 3.',
     see:'Crossover 2.46 rad/s, phase margin 74°, M_s = 1.06, and the dominant poles are real: ζ = 1, no overshoot. On the Bode plot the zero sits low (K_e v/K_θ ≈ 0.5 rad/s), so by the crossover the slope has long been −20 dB/decade and the phase is near −105°.',
     shows:'A heavily damped loop reads as a wide gap between the phase curve and −180° at crossover. The gain margin of 28 dB says the gain could rise 25-fold before instability, which is another way of saying the loop is nowhere near its limit.',
     predict:{q:'With a soft lateral gain k_e = 2 and k_θ = 3, what will the dominant poles look like?',opts:['Complex, ζ ≈ 0.4, visible overshoot','Real: ζ = 1, no overshoot, phase margin above 70°','Marginal: the soft gain cannot hold the path'],a:1}}],
   ['k_e = 12',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'rwf',rwKe:12,rwKth:3}},
    {setup:'The lateral gain raised to 12 with the same heading gain.',
     see:'The magnitude curve moves up, the crossover rises to 3.33 rad/s, and the zero moves up with it (K_e v/K_θ ≈ 3.2 rad/s), so at crossover the phase has lifted less: phase margin 41°, ζ = 0.41, predicted overshoot 24 %, M_s = 1.42. The pink |S| curve now peaks visibly above 0 dB near the crossover.',
     shows:'Raising the spring without the damper: the loop is faster and underdamped, and the Bode plot shows it as a crossover that has climbed toward the frequency where the phase is still falling. The rule of thumb ζ ≈ PM/100 gives 0.41, which is exactly the pole damping. A |S| peak above 0 dB means disturbances near that frequency are amplified, not rejected.',
     predict:{q:'Raising k_e from 2 to 12 with k_θ fixed: what happens to the phase margin and the |S| peak?',opts:['PM rises with the gain; |S| stays below 0 dB','No change in PM: the heading gain sets the damping','PM falls to about 40° and |S| peaks above 0 dB'],a:2}}],
   ['Measure L(jω) in the simulator',{test:'inj',injAmp:'small'},
    {setup:'Injects a small sinusoid at the actuator at nine frequencies, one at a time, in the full simulator, and measures the loop\'s response at each.',
     see:'Nine measured points land on the model\'s magnitude and phase curves, within about 0.1 dB and 1° at the lower frequencies and scattering a little at the top, where the response is small and one tick is a large fraction of a period.',
     shows:'The model is not an approximation someone chose; it is what the simulator does, measured. This is also how a real vehicle\'s loop is identified: inject, measure, fit. If the points left the curves, the model would be missing something, which the latency and lag lessons arrange on purpose.',
     predict:{q:'Where will the nine measured points land relative to the model curves?',opts:['On the curves, scattering a little at the top frequencies','Below the magnitude curve everywhere: the simulator loses gain','On the magnitude curve but about 25° off in phase'],a:0}}]],
  q:[['Why do two integrators give −40 dB per decade and −180°?','Each 1/s has magnitude 1/ω, which falls by a factor 10, −20 dB, per decade of frequency, and phase −90°. Two in series multiply the magnitudes and add the phases.'],
     ['What does the zero do, physically?','The zero is the heading feedback: the controller responds to ė as well as e, and at high frequency the ė term dominates, so the controller output leads the error. Lead is phase, and phase at crossover is margin. Without the zero the phase would sit at −180° plus the delay, and the loop could not be stabilized at all.'],
     ['M_s = 1.42 at k_e = 12. What does that number promise?','That the loop\'s Nyquist curve comes within 1/1.42 = 0.70 of −1, so the gain margin is at least 1/(1 − 0.70) = 3.4 (10.5 dB) and the phase margin at least 2 arcsin(0.70/2) = 41°, whichever direction the model error comes from. M_s is the one-number robustness summary, and above 2 the loop is fragile.']],
  check:{q:'A tracker with K_e = 4, K_θ = 2.5 at v₀ = 1.2 m/s: at what frequency is the controller\'s zero, K_e·v₀/K_θ, in rad/s?',a:1.92,tol:0.04,unit:'rad/s',how:'K_e v₀/K_θ = 4 × 1.2/2.5 = 1.92 rad/s.'}},
 {t:'Nyquist and the −1 point',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> The Bode plot shows margins; the Nyquist plot shows why they are margins, and it gives the one test that works even when the open loop is itself unstable or when the phase crosses −180° more than once, which delay makes happen. It is also the plot on which "a little more latency" becomes visible as a curve rotating into the forbidden point.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> The closed loop has a pole wherever 1 + <i>L</i>(<i>s</i>) = 0, so −1 is the point to stay away from. The plot traces <i>L</i>(jω) in the complex plane as ω runs from 0 to ∞ (solid) and its mirror image for negative frequencies (dashed). The Nyquist criterion counts the clockwise encirclements <i>N</i> of −1 by this curve and gives the number of unstable closed-loop poles as <i>Z</i> = <i>N</i> + <i>P</i>, with <i>P</i> the number of unstable open-loop poles, none here. The pink segment is the closest approach to −1; its length is 1/<i>M</i><sub>s</sub>. A delay T_d multiplies <i>L</i> by e<sup>−jωT_d</sup>, which rotates each point clockwise by ω<i>T</i><sub>d</sub> radians, more at higher frequency, so the outer part of the curve sweeps toward −1.</p>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The argument principle: as <i>s</i> traverses a contour enclosing the right half plane, the image of 1 + <i>L</i>(<i>s</i>) winds around the origin <i>Z</i> − <i>P</i> times; shifting by one makes that encirclements of −1 by <i>L</i>. Two integrators put a double pole on the contour, which is why the plot uses an indented path around the origin and, for display, the radius log₂(1 + |<i>L</i>|), which keeps −1 and the unit circle where they are while the curve at low frequency would otherwise be off the page. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/4-reading-the-margins">Derivation: chapter 6 §4</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The chips report <i>N</i>, <i>P</i> and <i>Z</i>, computed by integrating the argument along the contour, and cross-check <i>Z</i> against the eigenvalues of the discrete closed loop. The compressed/linear switch changes the radius scale. The three buttons here start from the fast pure-pursuit loop and add latency in whole 50 ms ticks, which is how the simulator adds it.</p>`,
  tries:[['Short lookahead, no latency',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.1,ppK:0,latency:0}},
    {setup:'The fast loop again: 0.1 m lookahead, crossover 17.6 rad/s, only the 25 ms hold delay.',
     see:'The curve passes to the right of −1 with its closest approach 1/1.65 = 0.6 away: N = 0, P = 0, Z = 0, stable, phase margin 40°, delay margin 0.04 s. The outer arc, the high-frequency part, is already rotated well clockwise by the hold delay.',
     shows:'Stable with little room: 40° of phase at 17.6 rad/s is 0.04 s of delay. The Nyquist curve makes the budget geometric: each point at frequency ω will rotate by ω × ΔT for an extra delay ΔT, and the point near crossover needs only 0.7 rad to reach −1.',
     predict:{q:'The fast loop with only the 25 ms hold delay: what will the Nyquist count say?',opts:['N = 0, stable, closest approach well inside the unit circle','N = 2: already unstable','N = 0, and the curve stays far from −1'],a:0}}],
   ['Short lookahead, 0.05 s latency',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.1,ppK:0,latency:0.05}},
    {setup:'The same fast loop with one tick of control latency, 50 ms, which is more than the 40 ms delay margin.',
     see:'The curve rotates through −1: N = 2, so Z = 2 unstable closed-loop poles, phase margin −10°, M_s = 6.9. The chips turn red and the poles chip shows a complex pair in the right half plane with ζ = −0.09.',
     shows:'Exactly what the delay margin promised: 50 ms is past 40 ms, and the loop is unstable. N = 2 rather than 1 because the pair of complex poles crosses together. The linear model now predicts an oscillation that grows without bound; the simulator, in the next lesson, shows what the turn-rate limit does with that.',
     predict:{q:'One tick of latency, 50 ms, against a delay margin of 40 ms: what will N be?',opts:['N = 0: one tick is small enough to absorb','N = 1: one real pole crosses','N = 2: a complex pair crosses together'],a:2}}],
   ['Short lookahead, 0.10 s latency',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.1,ppK:0,latency:0.1}},
    {setup:'The same fast loop with two ticks of latency.',
     see:'Still N = 2, Z = 2, phase margin −60°; the curve has rotated far past −1 and the unstable poles have moved further right (ζ = −0.35). M_s is no longer meaningful.',
     shows:'More delay does not add more unstable poles here, it makes the existing pair grow faster: the oscillation at roughly the crossover frequency doubles in amplitude every cycle instead of every few. The Nyquist count tells you whether; the pole positions tell you how fast.',
     predict:{q:'Doubling the latency to two ticks: what changes in the count and the poles?',opts:['N rises to 4: two more unstable poles','The curve rotates right round and N returns to 0','N stays 2; the unstable pair moves further right'],a:2}}]],
  q:[['The curve encircles −1 twice and the chips say Z = 2. What would a count of one mean?','A single real pole crossing into the right half plane: a loop that diverges without oscillating, which happens with a sign error or a gain so large that a real pole goes unstable. Oscillatory instability, the common case with delay, always comes as a complex pair and counts two.'],
     ['Why is the low-frequency part of the curve compressed?','Two integrators make |L| infinite as ω → 0, so the true curve shoots off to infinity along the negative real axis. The display maps the radius through log₂(1 + |L|), which leaves the unit circle and −1 in place and brings the tail back onto the screen; the linear switch shows the raw curve for comparison.'],
     ['Delay rotates the curve but leaves the magnitude alone. Why can it still make M_s huge?','M_s is the inverse distance to −1, and rotation moves the point nearest −1 along a circle of fixed radius |L|; when that circle passes through −1 the distance goes to zero. Magnitude unchanged, robustness gone: this is why delay is the dominant robustness problem in vehicle loops, not gain.']],
  check:{q:'An extra delay of 0.08 s is added to a loop. By how many degrees does the Nyquist point at ω = 6 rad/s rotate clockwise?',a:27.5,tol:0.6,unit:'°',how:'ω·ΔT = 6 × 0.08 = 0.48 rad = 0.48 × 180/π = 27.5°.'}},
 {t:'Delay margin: the latency budget',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Of all the margins, the delay margin is the one a vehicle engineer uses most, because latency is the thing most likely to change after the controller is tuned: a new camera, a busier CPU, a different message bus. It converts phase margin into a time, and a time can be budgeted across sensors, estimation, planning and actuation. This lesson shows the conversion, what the loop does when the budget is exceeded, and why a slower loop has a bigger one.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Phase margin is measured at the crossover frequency. An extra delay ΔT costs ω<sub>c</sub>·ΔT radians of phase there, so the loop can absorb</p>
<div class="eq">DM = PM / ω_c   (PM in radians)</div>
<p>of additional latency before the phase at crossover reaches −180° and the loop goes unstable. Fast loops have small budgets, because the same milliseconds are a larger fraction of a shorter period. Past the budget the linear model says the oscillation grows without bound; in the simulator the turn-rate limit caps the command, the effective gain drops, and the growth is bounded, which is what an unstable loop usually looks like on a real vehicle: a ringing that will not die, or a limit cycle.</p>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> e<sup>−jωΔ<i>T</i></sup> has unit magnitude and phase −ωΔ<i>T</i>; at ω = ω<sub>c</sub> the magnitude is still 1 and the phase margin becomes PM − ω<sub>c</sub>Δ<i>T</i>; it reaches zero at Δ<i>T</i> = PM/ω<sub>c</sub>. The delay margin is a first-order statement, because the crossover itself does not move with delay. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/4-reading-the-margins">Derivation: chapter 6 §4</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The simulator adds latency in whole 50 ms ticks, so the delay-margin chip tells you which setting tips the loop over. The step test runs the full nonlinear simulator with a 0.2 m (or 0.1 m) path offset and overlays the measured response on the linear prediction, so an unstable prediction and a bounded measurement can be compared directly.</p>`,
  tries:[['Fast loop (L<sub>0</sub> 0.1 m): small budget',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.1,ppK:0,latency:0}},
    {setup:'Pure pursuit with a 0.1 m lookahead, no latency.',
     see:'Phase margin 40.4° at 17.6 rad/s: 0.705 rad / 17.6 rad/s = 0.040 s of delay margin. The chip reads 0.04 s.',
     shows:'The budget is smaller than one control tick. Any sensor or network that adds 50 ms, which almost anything does, breaks this loop. A real stack would never run a lateral loop at 17 rad/s for exactly this reason; the lookahead would be lengthened until the budget covered the measured latency with room to spare.',
     predict:{q:'Phase margin 40° at 17.6 rad/s: roughly how much extra delay does that buy?',opts:['About 40 ms, less than one tick','About 0.4 s, several ticks','About 2 s'],a:0}}],
   ['Fast loop + 0.05 s latency, step test',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.1,ppK:0,latency:0.05},test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.1},
    {setup:'The same loop with one tick of latency, past its 40 ms budget, and a 0.1 m step test.',
     see:'The linear prediction (solid) grows without bound: unstable poles. The measured response (dashed amber) rings hard, overshoots about 8 times the predicted amount, and dies down over about 4 s; the message under the plot reports that the turn-rate limit was active.',
     shows:'Saturation is what separates "unstable" from "destroyed". When the command hits the turn-rate limit the loop gain effectively falls, the oscillation stops growing, and here it decays; with a little more delay it would settle into a sustained limit cycle instead. A linear analysis says the loop is unstable; the nonlinear vehicle shows how badly, and a limit cycle at the actuator limit is the symptom to recognize on a real vehicle.',
     predict:{q:'Past its delay budget by 10 ms, how will the simulated step respond?',opts:['It grows without bound, as the linear model says','It rings hard, but the turn-rate limit bounds it and it dies down','Normally: 10 ms over budget is within rounding'],a:1}}],
   ['Slow loop (L<sub>0</sub> 1.2 m), 0.3 s, step test',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:1.2,ppK:0,latency:0.3},test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.2},
    {setup:'A 1.2 m lookahead with 0.3 s of latency, six ticks, and a 0.2 m step test.',
     see:'Crossover 1.46 rad/s, phase margin 38°, and after absorbing 0.3 s the loop still has 0.46 s of delay margin left. The measured step overshoots 4.6 %, rises in 1.65 s and settles in 5 s, on top of the prediction.',
     shows:'The slow loop carries 0.3 s of latency, seven times the fast loop\'s entire budget, and is still comfortably stable, because its crossover is twelve times lower. Delay tolerance and bandwidth trade one for one. The end-to-end latency budget of a vehicle stack is set by this arithmetic: measure the latency, choose the crossover the margin allows, and tune the tracker to it.',
     predict:{q:'A 1.2 m lookahead carrying 0.3 s of latency, six ticks: what will the step show?',opts:['Unstable: 0.3 s is seven times the fast loop\'s whole budget','A limit cycle at the turn-rate limit','A clean, slow step with a few per cent overshoot and margin to spare'],a:2}}]],
  q:[['Why does the simulator\'s unstable response decay instead of growing?','Because the turn-rate limit clips the command. Describing-function reasoning: a saturated actuator acts like a gain that falls as the amplitude grows, so the amplitude rises until the effective gain brings the loop to the edge of stability, a limit cycle, or, when the clipped loop has margin to spare as here, the oscillation decays. Linear instability plus saturation is bounded, not safe.'],
     ['A stack measures 120 ms from camera exposure to steering command. What crossover can it afford with 45° of margin?','Keep at least 45° after the delay: if the undelayed loop has 65° (pure pursuit), 20° = 0.35 rad may go to delay, so ω_c ≤ 0.35/0.12 ≈ 2.9 rad/s. At 15 m/s with ω_c = 2.2 v/L_d, that is a lookahead of at least 11 m. Latency sets the lookahead.'],
     ['The delay margin chip is computed from the phase margin at one frequency. When is that misleading?','When the phase crosses −180° at several frequencies, or when the magnitude is near 0 dB over a range of frequencies, the first crossing is not the only one that matters and a delay can bring a different part of the curve onto −1 first. M_s catches those cases; the delay margin is the quick estimate and M_s the check.']],
  check:{q:'A loop has 36° of phase margin at a crossover of 4 rad/s. What is its delay margin, in seconds?',a:0.157,tol:0.01,unit:'s',how:'PM = 36° = 0.628 rad; DM = PM/ω_c = 0.628/4 = 0.157 s.'}},
 {t:'Poles, damping and the step response',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Margins say how far a loop is from instability; poles say what it does on the way there. The dominant closed-loop poles determine the overshoot, the settling time and the frequency of any ringing, and for a tracker those are what a passenger feels and what a lane-keeping specification is written in. Rear-wheel feedback has two gains that map directly onto the two pole parameters, which makes it the cleanest controller to learn this on.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> The closed-loop poles are where 1 + <i>L</i>(<i>s</i>) = 0, and the pair closest to the imaginary axis dominates the response. For a complex pair at −ζω<sub>n</sub> ± jω<sub>n</sub>√(1 − ζ²), the damping ratio ζ sets the overshoot and ζω<sub>n</sub> the settling rate. Rear-wheel feedback on the diff-drive, ignoring the small delay, is a mass-spring-damper: the lateral gain is the spring and the heading gain is the damper, so ω<sub>n</sub> = <i>v</i>₀√<i>k</i><sub>e</sub> and ζ = <i>k</i><sub>θ</sub>/(2√<i>k</i><sub>e</sub>). Keep <i>k</i><sub>e</sub> and turn <i>k</i><sub>θ</sub> and only the damping changes.</p>
<div class="eq">overshoot ≈ exp(−πζ / √(1−ζ²))       t_s(2%) ≈ 4 / (ζ·ω_n)
s² + v₀k_θ·s + v₀²k_e = 0   →   ω_n = v₀√k_e,   ζ = k_θ / (2√k_e)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> Substituting the law ω = <i>v</i>(−<i>k</i><sub>θ</sub>θ<sub>e</sub> − <i>k</i><sub>e</sub><i>e</i>) into the two integrators gives ë + <i>v</i><i>k</i><sub>θ</sub>ė + <i>v</i>²<i>k</i><sub>e</sub><i>e</i> = 0, the standard second-order form; the overshoot and settling formulas are its step response evaluated at the first peak and at the 2 % envelope. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch5/3-rear-wheel-feedback">The law and its Lyapunov argument: chapter 5 §3</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/5-poles-damping-and-the-shape-of-the-response">poles and the response: chapter 6 §5</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The poles come from the exact 20 Hz loop: each discrete pole <i>z</i> maps to <i>s</i> = ln <i>z</i>/<i>T</i>, so the delay is in them. The blue dots are the root locus, where the poles go if the controller\'s output is scaled from ×0.06 to ×16; where the locus enters the shaded right half plane, that scale factor is the gain margin. The step plot computes the response exactly rather than from the formulas, which the delay makes approximate; the step test then measures it in the simulator.</p>`,
  tries:[['k_θ = 1: light damping',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'rwf',rwKe:6,rwKth:1},test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.2},
    {setup:'Rear-wheel feedback with k_e = 6 and k_θ = 1 at 0.8 m/s, with a 0.2 m step test.',
     see:'The formulas give ω_n = 0.8√6 = 1.96 rad/s and ζ = 1/(2√6) = 0.20; the exact poles have ζ = 0.18, the delay costing the rest. Predicted overshoot 56 %, measured 55 %; settling 10 s. Phase margin 20°, M_s = 2.9.',
     shows:'A spring with almost no damper. The response rings at about 2 rad/s for ten seconds, and M_s near 3 says a small model error would make it ring for ever. The poles chip and the Bode plot describe the same fact; the step plot makes it visible as a shape.',
     predict:{q:'k_e = 6 with k_θ = 1: what overshoot will the step show?',opts:['Around 10 %','None: a low heading gain gives a slow, overdamped loop','Around 55 %, ringing for about ten seconds'],a:2}}],
   ['k_θ = 3',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'rwf',rwKe:6,rwKth:3},test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.2},
    {setup:'The same spring with the damper tripled.',
     see:'ζ = 0.61 (formula: 3/(2√6) = 0.61), predicted overshoot 9.1 %, measured 8.9 %, settling 3 s. Phase margin 56°, M_s = 1.1. The dominant poles have moved left along an arc of roughly constant ω_n.',
     shows:'The same bandwidth with the damping where it should be. This is the setting most trackers end up near: 5 to 10 % overshoot, margins in the 50s. Note what did not change: ω_n is set by k_e and the speed, so the settling time improved because ζ did, not because the loop got faster.',
     predict:{q:'Tripling the damper from k_θ = 1 to 3: what changes in the step?',opts:['Overshoot drops under 10 %; ω_n barely moves','The loop gets faster: ω_n rises with k_θ','Overshoot unchanged; only the ringing frequency drops'],a:0}}],
   ['k_θ = 6: heavy damping',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'rwf',rwKe:6,rwKth:6},test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.2},
    {setup:'The damper doubled again.',
     see:'The formula gives ζ = 1.22, overdamped; the exact poles are a real pair, ζ = 1 by convention, no overshoot measured, but the rise is slow, 2.3 s, and the settling 4.2 s is longer than at k_θ = 3. Phase margin 74°, crossover up to 4.9 rad/s because the heading feedback is now the loop\'s main term.',
     shows:'Past critical damping the response stops overshooting and starts dragging: one of the two real poles moves toward the origin and becomes the slow one. More damping is not more stability without limit; the crossover also rose, and with it the delay budget fell from 0.35 s to 0.26 s. The best settling is near ζ ≈ 0.7, which is where pure pursuit lives by construction.',
     predict:{q:'Doubling the damper again to k_θ = 6: what happens to the settling time?',opts:['It keeps improving: more damping, faster settling','No overshoot, but settling is longer than at k_θ = 3','The loop goes unstable from too much heading gain'],a:1}}]],
  q:[['The formula says ζ = 1.22 at k_θ = 6 but the chip says 1.0. Which is right?','Both, about different things. The formula is for the delay-free second-order model, where ζ > 1 means two distinct real poles. The exact discrete loop has those two real poles plus the delay\'s own dynamics; the chip reports the dominant pole and calls any real pole ζ = 1. Overdamped is overdamped either way; the number past 1 has no shape to describe.'],
     ['Why does the root locus use a gain scale of ×0.06 to ×16 rather than the gains themselves?','The locus asks one question: how do the poles move if the whole controller is scaled, which is what an unknown plant gain (tyres, a different speed) effectively does. Where the locus crosses into the right half plane is the gain margin as a factor. Scaling k_e and k_θ separately would be two loci and two questions.'],
     ['On the car, what plays the role of v₀²k_e?','The same spring, multiplied by the plant gain g = v₀/L: ω_n = v₀√(g k_e). The car\'s loop stiffens with speed faster than the diff-drive\'s, which is the subject of the gain-scheduling lesson and of chapter 1, where speed also changes the vehicle.']],
  check:{q:'Rear-wheel feedback on the diff-drive at v₀ = 1 m/s with k_e = 4 and k_θ = 2: what is the 2 % settling time t_s ≈ 4/(ζ·ω_n), in seconds?',a:4,tol:0.1,unit:'s',how:'ω_n = v₀√k_e = 1 × 2 = 2 rad/s; ζ = k_θ/(2√k_e) = 2/4 = 0.5; t_s = 4/(0.5 × 2) = 4 s.'}},
 {t:'Actuator lag, and what MPC assumes',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Actuators are not instantaneous. A steering motor, a hydraulic valve, a motor controller with its own inner loop: each follows its command with a time constant, and that lag costs phase exactly like delay does, frequency by frequency. For a model-based controller the question is sharper: does the controller\'s model include the lag? An MPC that believes its steering is instantaneous plans as if its commands took effect at once and then reacts to the lagging actuator it measures, and the cost is paid in robustness before it is paid in tracking error. This lesson is the first place in the lab where "the controller is only as good as its model" is a number.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> A first-order actuator 1/(τ<i>s</i> + 1) contributes magnitude 1/√(1 + ω²τ²) and phase −atan(ωτ): at ω = 2 rad/s with τ = 0.15 s that is 17° of phase, a quarter of a typical margin. Pure pursuit simply loses that phase. The MPC without the lag in its model does worse than losing phase: its gains were optimized for a plant that responds instantly, so they are too aggressive for the real one, and the linearized loop shows it. With the lag modeled the MPC also feeds back its own commanded value (the <i>K</i><sub>c</sub> gain in the block diagram), anticipating what the actuator will do, and the margin comes back.</p>
<p>As implemented, with <i>u</i> the actuator's state (the speed, and the turn rate or the steering angle) and <i>u</i><sub>cmd</sub> its command after saturation; the acceleration and steering-rate limits act after the lag. The MPC's inputs are the rates <i>u̇</i> = (<i>a</i>, rate of steering), applied over its 0.1 s step.</p>
<div class="eq">τ·u̇ = u_cmd − u   →   1/(τs + 1):   magnitude 1/√(1 + ω²τ²),   phase −atan(ωτ)
simulator, every 50 ms:  u ← u + (u_cmd − u)·(1 − e^(−Δt/τ))
MPC, lag modeled:        u_{k+1} = u_k + (u_cmd,k − u_k)·(1 − e^(−Δt/τ)),   u_cmd,k+1 = u_cmd,k + u̇_k·Δt
MPC, lag not modeled:    u_{k+1} = u_k + u̇_k·Δt   (each command takes effect at once)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The lag is a pole at <i>s</i> = −1/τ in <i>L</i>(<i>s</i>); the MPC\'s gains, including <i>K</i><sub>c</sub>, are read from the solver by finite differences at the operating point, and the lag model adds the commanded steering as a state the MPC predicts through a first-order response. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch7/2-the-prediction-model">The lag model: chapter 7 §2</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch7/6-what-the-analyze-view-sees">how the MPC becomes gains: §6</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/3-the-open-loop-l-s-with-the-clock-in-it">the lag in L(s): chapter 6 §3</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The actuator lag τ is a Vehicle-tab slider and applies to both speed and steering (turn rate). The MPC\'s "Include actuator lag" checkbox in the Control tab adds the lag to its prediction model. Margin and tracking performance are different things: run L5 both ways and the cross-track RMS barely changes; what changes is the margin.</p>`,
  tries:[['Pure pursuit, τ = 0.15 s',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',lag:0.15}},
    {setup:'Pure pursuit on the diff-drive with a 0.15 s actuator time constant.',
     see:'Phase margin 48.8° against 62.7° without the lag: the 14° difference is the lag\'s phase at the crossover, atan(1.89 × 0.15) = 15.8° at 1.89 rad/s, less the 2° the loop wins back because the crossover slides down from 1.95 rad/s. Delay margin down from 0.56 to 0.45 s, M_s up from 1.05 to 1.32.',
     shows:'Lag is delay-like but frequency dependent: it costs little at low frequency and up to 90° at high, and it also attenuates, which is why the gain margin went up while the phase margin went down. A tracker with no model of its actuator simply absorbs the loss, and the lookahead has to be chosen with the lag included.',
     predict:{q:'A 0.15 s actuator lag on pure pursuit at about 1.9 rad/s: what happens to the two margins?',opts:['Phase margin and gain margin both fall','Nothing visible: 0.15 s is well below the 0.56 s delay margin','Phase margin falls about 14°; gain margin rises'],a:2}}],
   ['MPC, τ = 0.15 s, lag not modeled',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'mpc',lag:0.15,mpcLag:false}},
    {setup:'The MPC on the same lagging vehicle, with its prediction model assuming an instantaneous actuator.',
     see:'Phase margin 29.7°, M_s = 2.04, delay margin 0.16 s, gain margin 19.7 dB. Worse than pure pursuit on the same plant by 19° of phase, and past the M_s = 2 line that marks a fragile loop.',
     shows:'The optimizer did its job on the wrong plant. It chose gains for a vehicle that steers at once, which on a vehicle that steers over 0.15 s means it commands, sees nothing happen, commands harder, and then the actuator catches up with everything at once. The tracking error in a benign run looks fine; the margin says it would not survive an extra 160 ms of latency or a modest model error.',
     predict:{q:'The MPC on the lagging vehicle with no lag in its model: how will its margin compare with pure pursuit on the same plant?',opts:['Better: the optimizer compensates through feedback','About the same: lag costs every controller the same phase','Worse, with M_s past 2'],a:2}}],
   ['MPC, τ = 0.15 s, lag modeled',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'mpc',lag:0.15,mpcLag:true}},
    {setup:'The same MPC with the lag included in its prediction model.',
     see:'Phase margin 52.8°, M_s = 1.25, delay margin 0.24 s. The block diagram now shows the K_c feedback from the commanded steering, and the analysis reports a crossover of 3.9 rad/s.',
     shows:'With the right model the MPC is both faster than pure pursuit (3.9 against 1.9 rad/s) and better damped than the unmodeled version. The feedback on its own command is the mechanism: the controller knows what it has already asked for and does not ask again. This is the general lesson of model-based control: the model is part of the controller, and the margins measure the model as much as the gains.',
     predict:{q:'Adding the lag to the MPC\'s model: what will the analysis show?',opts:['Margin recovers above 50° and the crossover is above pure pursuit\'s','Margin recovers but the loop is slower than pure pursuit','No change: the gains are the same, only the prediction differs'],a:0}}]],
  q:[['Why does the gain margin improve with the lag while the phase margin worsens?','The lag attenuates: its magnitude 1/√(1 + ω²τ²) falls at high frequency, so where the phase reaches −180° the magnitude is lower than before, which is a larger gain margin. The phase loss at crossover is the cost; the attenuation above crossover is a side benefit. Lag and pure delay differ exactly here, since delay attenuates nothing.'],
     ['The cross-track error barely changes between the two MPC runs. Why worry?','Because tracking error on a known plant in a simulator is the best case. Margin measures what happens on a plant that differs from the model, which every real plant does, by tyre wear, load, temperature and the lag itself changing. A loop at M_s = 2 tracks fine until the day the actuator is 30 % slower, and then it rings. Margin is what a safety case is written in.'],
     ['What would the lag model need on a real vehicle?','An identified time constant, which can be measured by stepping the command and timing the response (the speed-loop lesson does exactly this), and a check that it is constant: hydraulic and electric steering lags change with load and temperature. If it varies, the MPC should model the slow end and the margin analysis the fast one.']],
  check:{q:'A first-order actuator with τ = 0.2 s: how many degrees of phase does it cost at ω = 3 rad/s?',a:30.96,tol:0.6,unit:'°',how:'phase = atan(ωτ) = atan(3 × 0.2) = atan(0.6) = 30.96°.'}},
 {t:'Integral action and steady-state error',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Real actuators are biased: a steering link a degree off centre, one motor a little weaker, a miscalibrated zero. A proportional-derivative loop reacts to such a bias by settling where the controller\'s output cancels it, which needs a standing error. For a lane-keeping controller that is a vehicle that drives steadily a few centimetres off the centre line, for ever. Integral action removes it, and the price it charges, phase, is the subject of the second half of this lesson. MPC gets the same effect from its structure, with a condition that is easy to miss.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> A constant actuator bias is a step disturbance at the plant input. With only proportional and derivative terms the loop settles where the controller output equals minus the bias, and the error that produces that output is <i>e</i><sub>ss</sub> = <i>d</i>/<i>K</i><sub>e</sub>: larger bias or softer gain, larger standing error. An integrator keeps adding to the command as long as the error is nonzero, so the only equilibrium is zero error. In the frequency domain the integrator is 1/<i>s</i>: infinite gain at zero frequency (which is what kills the steady error) and −90° of phase at low frequency, which eats margin if the integral gain is large enough to matter near the crossover. The MPC commands a rate and builds each command on its previous one, so it integrates its input and removes a bias it can see.</p>
<div class="eq">e_ss = d / K_e     (K_e: command per metre of lateral error)
PID tracker:  u = K_p·α + K_i·∫α dt + K_d·α̇,   α: bearing to the carrot,  ∫α dt clamped to ±1 rad·s
MPC:          u_k = u_{k−1} + u̇_k·Δt   (it commands a rate, so its command is an integral)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> At steady state ė = θ̇<sub>e</sub> = 0, so the plant input must be zero: controller output + bias = 0, and with output −<i>K</i><sub>e</sub><i>e</i> that is <i>e</i> = <i>d</i>/<i>K</i><sub>e</sub>. With an integrator, a constant nonzero <i>e</i> would make the output grow without bound, so steady state requires <i>e</i> = 0. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/5-poles-damping-and-the-shape-of-the-response">The bias response: chapter 6 §5</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch7/7-why-it-integrates">why the MPC integrates: chapter 7 §7</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch5/5-pid-on-the-bearing">the PID tracker: chapter 5 §5</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The response plot switches to the bias case here: the plotted quantity is the lateral error per unit of bias, so a final value of 0.28 means 28 % of the bias-response unit remains. The Vehicle tab has the same bias as a slider for normal runs: a steering misalignment in degrees for the car, a turn-rate offset in rad/s for the diff-drive. The MPC measures the diff-drive\'s turn rate directly; the car\'s steering encoder reads the angle before the misaligned link.</p>`,
  tries:[['Car, rear-wheel feedback, 2° bias',{level:LV_CAR,set:{ctl:'rwf'},stepKind:'bias',test:'step'},
    {setup:'The car with rear-wheel feedback (k_e = 6, k_θ = 3) and a 2° steering misalignment, applied as a step.',
     see:'The error rises to a peak of 0.30 and settles in 4 s at 0.28 of the bias unit: a standing offset that never goes away. The linear prediction and the measured response agree.',
     shows:'PD control at work: the loop is stable, well damped, and permanently off the path by d/K_e. On a real car this is the drift that the driver corrects by holding the wheel slightly off centre; a controller without integral action does the same, with the error instead of the wheel.',
     predict:{q:'Rear-wheel feedback under a 2° misalignment: where will the error settle?',opts:['At zero, after a transient','At a standing offset that never goes away','It drifts away: the bias wins'],a:1}}],
   ['Diff-drive MPC, 0.1 rad/s bias',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'mpc'},stepKind:'bias',test:'step'},
    {setup:'The diff-drive with the MPC and a 0.1 rad/s turn-rate bias.',
     see:'A transient peak of 0.24, then the error returns to within 0.2 % of zero in under 4 s. Phase margin 53°, unchanged by the bias.',
     shows:'The MPC\'s rate input integrates the bias away: it keeps asking for a little more counter-turn until the measured turn rate matches the plan, and because it measures the turn rate the bias is visible to it. No integral gain was tuned; the structure did it. The settling time is the same as the step response, because the integration happens in the command, not through a slow extra pole.',
     predict:{q:'The MPC, with no integral gain tuned, under a turn-rate bias: what residual will remain?',opts:['Essentially zero: the rate input integrates it away','The same d/K_e as a PD loop','A slow creep lasting tens of seconds'],a:0}}],
   ['Car, PID with K_i = 0',{level:LV_CAR,set:{ctl:'pid',kp:2,ki:0,kd:0.15},stepKind:'bias',test:'step'},
    {setup:'The car with PID on the bearing, proportional and derivative only, under the same 2° misalignment.',
     see:'Peak 0.30, settled in 4.3 s at 0.29: the same standing error as rear-wheel feedback, because the proportional gain on the bearing plays the role of K_e. Phase margin 72°.',
     shows:'PD is PD whatever it is called. The 29 % residual is d/K_e for this controller\'s effective lateral gain; the only way to remove it without changing the gain is to add a term that keeps acting while the error stands.',
     predict:{q:'PID on the bearing with K_i = 0 under the same 2° bias: how does its residual compare with rear-wheel feedback?',opts:['Smaller: the bearing already encodes the heading','Zero: the derivative term handles it','About the same standing error, near 30 %'],a:2}}],
   ['Car, PID with K_i = 0.4',{level:LV_CAR,set:{ctl:'pid',kp:2,ki:0.4,kd:0.15},stepKind:'bias',test:'step'},
    {setup:'K_i raised to 0.4.',
     see:'The residual falls to 0.9 %, but the response is now a slow creep: peak 0.235, then a long tail that takes 16 s to settle within 2 %. Phase margin 66°, a few degrees lower, and a slow real pole has appeared near the origin.',
     shows:'The integrator removed the offset and added a slow mode: the integral has to wind up to the value that cancels the bias, and at k_i = 0.4 that takes a quarter of a minute. A larger k_i winds up faster and costs more phase at crossover; the usual compromise is to make the integral slow (tens of seconds) and accept the creep, or to use the MPC\'s structural integration, which has no such tail. Integral action only fixes what it can see: the car\'s steering misalignment is downstream of the steering encoder, so the MPC on the car leaves a small residual where the diff-drive MPC leaves none.',
     predict:{q:'Adding K_i = 0.4: what happens to the residual and the settling time?',opts:['Residual under 1 %, settling stretches to tens of seconds','Residual under 1 % at no cost in settling','Residual halves; the loop rings from the lost phase'],a:0}}]],
  q:[['Why does the MPC on the car not remove the misalignment completely?','Its integrator acts on the steering it measures, and the encoder reads the angle before the misaligned link, so the measured steering is correct and the vehicle turns anyway. What the MPC can integrate is the pose error through its tracking terms, which is slower and leaves a residual. An integrator removes the bias it can observe; sensor placement decides which biases those are.'],
     ['Where does the slow tail at k_i = 0.4 come from?','From a new closed-loop pole near s = −k_i/k_p-ish, introduced by the integrator. The integral state must reach the value that cancels the bias, and it charges at a rate set by k_i times the error; small k_i means a slow charge. The pole is real and slow, so it adds a creep rather than a ring.'],
     ['A standing 28 % residual: in metres, what is that for a 2° misalignment at 0.6 m/s?','The bias unit is the lateral error per unit of actuator bias with the loop\'s gains; for this car and these gains 2° of steering is roughly 2 cm of standing offset. Small, and permanent. At highway speed the same misalignment would be tens of centimetres, which is why every production lane-keeping loop has integral action or an explicit bias estimate.']],
  check:{q:'A PD tracker with lateral gain K_e = 2.5 rad/s per metre faces a constant turn-rate bias d = 0.1 rad/s. What standing error does it settle at, in cm?',a:4,tol:0.1,unit:'cm',how:'e_ss = d/K_e = 0.1/2.5 = 0.04 m = 4 cm.'}},
 {t:'Linear versus nonlinear',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Everything in the Analyze view is linear: gains read at one operating point, responses that scale with the input. The vehicle is not. Steering locks, steering-rate limits, turn-rate and acceleration limits all exist, and a large enough error asks for more than the actuator can give. Knowing where the linear picture stops being true is as important as the picture itself, because the failures that matter on a real vehicle, a swerve that saturates the steering, a limit cycle after a big disturbance, live exactly there.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> For small signals the response is proportional to the input, so a 0.05 m step and a 1 m step have the same shape and the Bode plot describes both. A large step asks the controller for a large command, the actuator clips it, and the response stops scaling: it is slower and less overshooting than the linear model predicts, because the clipped actuator is effectively a smaller gain. The same happens in the frequency domain: a large injected sine saturates the rate limit at high frequency, and the measured points bend away from the model curve where the saturation bites.</p>
<div class="eq">saturation:  u = clamp(u_cmd, −u_max, u_max)        rate limit:  |u_k − u_{k−1}| ≤ r_max·Δt
car: |δ| ≤ 32°, |δ̇| ≤ 1.5 rad/s;   diff-drive: |ω| ≤ 1.8 rad/s, |ω̇| ≤ 3 rad/s² with acceleration limits on   (defaults)
describing function of saturation, for a sine of amplitude A > u_max:
   N(A) = (2/π)·[asin(u_max/A) + (u_max/A)·√(1 − (u_max/A)²)]      (N = 1 for A ≤ u_max)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> Saturation is a static nonlinearity; its describing function is a gain that falls with amplitude, from 1 for small signals toward zero for large ones. A loop with a saturating actuator behaves, for sinusoidal inputs, like the linear loop with that reduced gain, which is why large-amplitude measurements fall below the model and why the step response slows. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/7-where-the-model-stops">Where the model stops: chapter 6 §7</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch1/6-the-limits-and-where-the-kinematic-model-stops">the vehicle\'s limits: chapter 1 §6</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The car\'s steering moves at most 1.5 rad/s and locks at 32° (Vehicle tab); the diff-drive\'s turn rate and acceleration are limited too. The step test accepts amplitudes from 0.05 to 1 m and the injection has a small and a large setting; the message under the experiments reports when a limit was active and for how long.</p>`,
  tries:[['Car, 0.05 m step test',{level:LV_CAR,set:{ctl:'rwf'},stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.05,test:'step'},
    {setup:'The car with rear-wheel feedback and a 5 cm path offset.',
     see:'Measured overshoot 9.0 % against 9.0 % predicted, rise 1.25 s, settled in 4.05 s; the dashed measurement lies on the solid prediction. No limit reported.',
     shows:'Small signal: the linear model is exact to the width of the line. This is the regime every chip in the Analyze view describes.',
     predict:{q:'A 5 cm step on the car: how will the measurement compare with the prediction?',opts:['On it, to the width of the line, no limit active','Slower, with the steering-rate limit active','Faster: small steps are easier than the model says'],a:0}}],
   ['Car, 1.0 m step test',{level:LV_CAR,set:{ctl:'rwf'},stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:1.0,test:'step'},
    {setup:'The same loop with a 1 m offset, twenty times larger.',
     see:'The measured response is slower, rise 2.0 s instead of 1.25, and overshoots less, 5.3 % instead of 9 %; the plot is normalized per metre, so the shapes can be compared directly, and they differ. The message reports the steering-rate limit active during the first part of the response.',
     shows:'The controller asked for a steering angle it could not get fast enough; the steering ramped at 1.5 rad/s while the error waited, and by the time it caught up the error was smaller than the linear loop would have had it, so the overshoot is smaller. A saturated loop is slower and, up to a point, better damped. The danger is the other direction: a saturated loop with delay rings, and that is the limit cycle of the delay-margin lesson.',
     predict:{q:'Twenty times the step, 1 m: how will the normalized response differ?',opts:['Same shape: the response scales with the input','Faster rise and more overshoot, rate limit active','Slower rise and less overshoot, rate limit active'],a:2}}],
   ['Car, large-sine L(jω)',{level:LV_CAR,set:{ctl:'rwf'},injAmp:'large',test:'inj'},
    {setup:'The injection experiment with a large sine amplitude.',
     see:'At low frequency the measured points still sit on the model. From a few rad/s up they fall below the magnitude curve and the phase scatters, and the message reports the steering-rate limit active at those frequencies.',
     shows:'The rate limit is a slew limit: a sine of amplitude A at frequency ω needs a rate Aω, so the same amplitude saturates at high frequency and not at low. The measured magnitude is the describing-function gain, falling where Aω exceeds 1.5 rad/s. On a real vehicle this is how you find the amplitude beyond which the Bode plot stops applying.',
     predict:{q:'Injecting a large sine: where will the measured points leave the model curves?',opts:['At low frequency, where the excursion is largest','At high frequency, where the rate limit bites','Nowhere: the injection amplitude does not matter'],a:1}}]],
  q:[['Why does saturation reduce overshoot here but cause limit cycles elsewhere?','Saturation lowers the effective gain. In a loop with plenty of phase margin a lower gain is more damped, hence less overshoot. In a loop past its delay margin the lower gain brings the loop back toward the stability boundary rather than through it, so the oscillation neither grows nor dies: a limit cycle. Same mechanism, different starting margin.'],
     ['The 1 m response is normalized per metre and looks "better". Is a saturated loop safer?','No. It is slower, which on a curve means a larger excursion before the correction catches up, and the saturation means the controller has no authority left for anything else that happens during the ramp. A planner that leaves the tracker steering margin (the Hybrid A* 85 % rule) exists so that the tracker stays in the linear regime.'],
     ['What does a describing function assume, and when does it fail?','That the loop filters out the harmonics the nonlinearity generates, so only the fundamental matters. It fails when the loop passes harmonics, which is rare for these low-pass vehicle loops, and it cannot predict anything about non-sinusoidal behaviour such as the first swerve after a step, which the time-domain simulation shows directly.']],
  check:{q:'An actuator saturates at u_max = 1 and is driven by a sine of amplitude A = 2. What is the describing-function gain N(A) of the saturation?',a:0.609,tol:0.012,unit:'',how:'u_max/A = 0.5; N = (2/π)[asin(0.5) + 0.5·√(1 − 0.25)] = (2/π)(0.5236 + 0.4330) = 0.609.'}},
 {t:'Speed changes the plant: gain scheduling',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> A vehicle is not one plant; it is a family of plants indexed by speed. The same steering angle turns the car twice as fast at twice the speed, and the same heading error grows the lateral error twice as fast. A controller tuned at 10 m/s is a different loop at 30 m/s, with a different crossover and different margins. Gain scheduling, changing the controller with speed so the loop stays the same, is the standard answer, and pure pursuit contains a built-in version of it.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> The plant gain is <i>v</i>₀ for the diff-drive and <i>v</i>₀²/<i>L</i> for the car: both the turn rate (<i>v</i> tan δ/<i>L</i>) and the lateral rate (<i>v</i>θ<sub>e</sub>) scale with speed. Fixed gains therefore give a loop whose crossover rises with speed, and a crossover that rises walks into the delay limit. Pure pursuit schedules itself: L_d = L<sub>0</sub> + k·v₀ grows with speed, so ω<sub>c</sub> = 2.2 <i>v</i>₀/<i>L</i><sub>d</sub> levels off at 2.2/<i>k</i> as speed rises. Rear-wheel feedback works in curvature, so its turn-rate gain is <i>v</i> times the curvature gain and its crossover grows roughly in proportion to speed unless the gains are scheduled.</p>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> With ω<sub>n</sub> = <i>v</i>₀√(<i>g</i><i>K</i><sub>e</sub>) and <i>K</i><sub>e</sub> = 2<i>v</i>₀/<i>L</i><sub>d</sub>² for pure pursuit, ω<sub>n</sub> = √2 <i>v</i>₀/<i>L</i><sub>d</sub>; put <i>L</i><sub>d</sub> = <i>L</i>₀ + <i>k</i><i>v</i>₀ and the ratio tends to √2/<i>k</i> as <i>v</i>₀ grows. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/2-pure-pursuit-is-a-pd-controller-in-disguise">Derivation: chapter 6 §2</a> · what else changes with speed in the vehicle itself: <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch1/5-understeer-the-characteristic-speed-and-stability">chapter 1 §5</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The operating speed of the analysis is the cruise speed (capped by <i>v</i><sub>max</sub>), both in the Control tab. The buttons change the cruise speed with the lookahead\'s speed term off and on. The kinematic vehicle here never loses grip, so the plant gain keeps growing with speed without limit; chapter 1 describes what a real vehicle does instead, and the car with tyre slip (Vehicle tab) does it: <a class="lsref" href="#lesson=ctl:16">Gain scheduling breaks above v_ch</a> repeats these buttons on it.</p>`,
  tries:[['PP, k = 0, v = 0.4',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.5,ppK:0,vcruise:0.4}},
    {setup:'Pure pursuit with a fixed 0.5 m lookahead (no speed term) at 0.4 m/s.',
     see:'Crossover 1.76 rad/s (2.2 × 0.4/0.5), phase margin 63°, delay margin 0.63 s.',
     shows:'At low speed a fixed lookahead gives a slow, comfortable loop. Note the plant gain: v₀ = 0.4 enters K_e = 2v₀/L_d² directly.',
     predict:{q:'A fixed 0.5 m lookahead at 0.4 m/s: roughly what crossover?',opts:['About 0.4 rad/s','About 1.8 rad/s','About 7 rad/s'],a:1}}],
   ['PP, k = 0, v = 1.6',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.5,ppK:0,vcruise:1.6,vmax:1.6}},
    {setup:'The same fixed lookahead at four times the speed.',
     see:'Crossover 7.03 rad/s, four times higher, phase margin 55.5°, delay margin 0.14 s, gain margin 19.5 dB. The loop has moved most of the way to its delay limit without a single controller setting changing.',
     shows:'Speed is a gain. With the lookahead fixed, the crossover is proportional to v₀, the delay budget inversely, and a loop that had 0.63 s of budget at walking pace has 0.14 s at running pace. On a real vehicle this is the difference between a lane-keeping loop that is fine in town and one that rings on the motorway.',
     predict:{q:'The same fixed lookahead at four times the speed: what happens to the crossover and the delay margin?',opts:['Crossover ×4, delay margin about ÷4','Crossover unchanged: the lookahead sets it','Crossover ×2, since the plant gain goes as √v'],a:0}}],
   ['PP, k = 0.5, v = 1.6',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.5,ppK:0.5,vcruise:1.6,vmax:1.6}},
    {setup:'The lookahead\'s speed term restored: L_d = 0.5 + 0.5 × 1.6 = 1.3 m at 1.6 m/s.',
     see:'Crossover 2.7 rad/s, phase margin 61.7°, delay margin 0.40 s: nearly the low-speed loop back, at four times the speed.',
     shows:'The speed term is a gain schedule. L_d growing with v keeps 2.2 v₀/L_d bounded by 2.2/k = 4.4 rad/s however fast the robot goes, and the margin stays where the tuning put it. Rear-wheel feedback and PID need the same treatment explicitly, gains divided by speed; MPC gets it from its model, which has the speed in it.',
     predict:{q:'Restoring the speed term, L_d = 0.5 + 0.5v, at 1.6 m/s: where will the loop land?',opts:['Faster still: a longer lookahead at speed adds gain','Unchanged from the k = 0 run at 1.6 m/s','Near the low-speed loop: crossover under 3 rad/s, margin above 60°'],a:2}}]],
  q:[['Why does the crossover level off at 2.2/k rather than keep falling?','ω_c = 2.2 v₀/(L₀ + k v₀); as v₀ grows the L₀ becomes negligible and the ratio tends to 2.2/k. The loop\'s bandwidth becomes a constant set by k alone, which is the designer\'s choice of how fast the loop should be at speed.'],
     ['How would you schedule rear-wheel feedback?','Its loop has ω_n = v₀√k_e and ζ = k_θ/(2√k_e), so dividing k_e by v₀² and k_θ by v₀ keeps both constant. In practice the gains are tabulated against speed and interpolated, which is what gain scheduling means on a production vehicle, with the table checked at every breakpoint by exactly the analysis in this view.'],
     ['The kinematic plant gain grows with speed without limit. What stops it on a real car?','The tyres. Above a characteristic speed the lateral force needed for a given curvature exceeds what the tyres supply linearly, the vehicle understeers, and the steering-to-yaw gain falls instead of rising. The kinematic model used here is valid at low lateral acceleration; chapter 1 derives the dynamic bicycle model that takes over.']],
  check:{q:'Pure pursuit with L_d = L₀ + k·v₀, L₀ = 1 m and k = 0.6 s, at v₀ = 20 m/s: what is the crossover ω_c ≈ 2.2 v₀/L_d, in rad/s?',a:3.385,tol:0.07,unit:'rad/s',how:'L_d = 1 + 0.6 × 20 = 13 m; ω_c = 2.2 × 20/13 = 3.385 rad/s.'}},
];
const LESSONS_EXTRA=[
 {t:'The speed loop is open, except for MPC',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Everything so far was the lateral loop. Speed is the other axis, and in most of this lab it is not a loop at all: the trackers command a speed from a profile and nothing measures the actual speed and corrects the command. That is common on small robots, where a motor controller does the closing underneath, and it is worth seeing what the open chain looks like, because the actuator chain, latency, lag and acceleration limit, is the same one the lateral loop drives through. MPC is the exception: its model has speed as a state, so it closes a speed loop of its own, with margins of its own.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Every path tracker here commands speed from a profile: cruise speed, slowed in curves by the lateral-acceleration cap, and ramped down toward the goal. The Speed plot shows the actuator chain following it: a delay T_lat, then a first-order lag 1/(τ<i>s</i> + 1) with bandwidth about 1/τ and a 10–90 % rise time of 2.2τ, then the acceleration limit. The MPC instead penalizes (<i>v</i> − <i>v</i><sub>ref</sub>)² in its cost and commands acceleration, so the speed loop has gains the analysis can read, <i>K</i><sub>v</sub> on the speed error, <i>K</i><sub>vc</sub> on the commanded speed when the lag is modeled, <i>K</i><sub>u</sub> on its previous acceleration, and the same crossover, margins and step response as the lateral loop.</p>
<div class="eq">G(s) = e^{−sT_lat} / (τs + 1)        bandwidth ≈ 1/τ,  10–90 % rise ≈ 2.2 τ
MPC:  a = −K_v (v − v_ref) − K_vc (v_cmd − v_ref) + K_u a_prev   closes a loop through its rate input
profile:  v_ref = min(v_cruise, v_max, √(a_lat / |κ|), √(2a·(d_goal − 0.08 m)))     κ: curvature just ahead (pure pursuit: its own)
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The first-order lag\'s step response is 1 − e<sup>−<i>t</i>/τ</sup>; it reaches 10 % at 0.105τ and 90 % at 2.3τ, hence 2.2τ between them, and its magnitude falls to 1/√2 at ω = 1/τ. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch5/1-path-coordinates-and-the-speed-profile">The speed profile: chapter 5 §1</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch7/8-the-speed-loop">the MPC\'s speed loop: chapter 7 §8</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> Switch the loop selector in the Analyze view to Speed. For pure pursuit the chips report the plant\'s bandwidth and time constant and the step test lands on the open-chain prediction. For MPC the loop has margins of its own, and the step test shows what its acceleration bound costs when the step is large. The speed step test raises the cruise speed by 0.2 m/s in the full simulator.</p>`,
  tries:[['Pure pursuit, τ = 0.3 s',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',lag:0.3},loop:'spd',test:'spdstep'},
    {setup:'Pure pursuit with a 0.3 s actuator lag; the Speed loop view and a 0.2 m/s speed step.',
     see:'The chips report an open chain with bandwidth 3.4 rad/s, the inverse of τ, and no margins, because there is no loop. The step rises from 10 to 90 % in 0.65 s (2.2 × 0.3 = 0.66) and settles in 1.2 s with no overshoot; the measured response lies on the prediction.',
     shows:'An open chain does what the actuator does: an exponential approach with the actuator\'s own time constant. Nothing can make it faster than τ allows, and nothing corrects it if the actuator is slow or biased. This is also a measurement of τ: step the command, time the response, and the lag model for the MPC is identified.',
     predict:{q:'A speed step through an open chain with τ = 0.3 s: what will the chips and the step show?',opts:['Margins like the lateral loop, and some overshoot','No margins; a 10–90 % rise of about 0.65 s, no overshoot','A rise of about 0.3 s, the time constant'],a:1}}],
   ['MPC with the lag modeled',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'mpc',lag:0.3,mpcLag:true},loop:'spd',test:'spdstep'},
    {setup:'The MPC on the same lagging actuator, lag in its model, Speed loop view, 0.2 m/s step.',
     see:'Now there are margins: crossover 3.1 rad/s, phase margin 71°, delay margin 0.40 s, M_s = 1.15, ζ = 0.83. The step rises in the same 0.65 s, overshoots 1 % and settles in 1.1 s.',
     shows:'A closed loop around the same actuator reaches the reference in the same time, because the actuator still sets the pace, but it is now a loop: a biased or slow actuator would be corrected, and the price is the usual one, margins to keep. The MPC\'s speed gains come from the same cost weights as its lateral gains, so Q_v is where this loop is tuned.',
     predict:{q:'The MPC closing a speed loop around the same actuator: how fast will the step rise compared with the open chain?',opts:['Much faster: feedback beats the lag','About the same, and now there are margins','Slower: the loop adds phase lag'],a:1}}],
   ['Latency 0.2 s',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',lag:0.3,latency:0.2},loop:'spd',test:'spdstep'},
    {setup:'Pure pursuit with the lag and 0.2 s of control latency.',
     see:'The same exponential, shifted right by 0.2 s: rise still 0.65 s, settling 1.4 s instead of 1.2. No margin chips, because an open chain has nothing to destabilize.',
     shows:'Latency in an open chain is just lateness. The same 0.2 s in the lateral loop would cost 0.2 × ω_c radians of phase and could destabilize it; here it costs nothing but time. The difference is the feedback: delay is only dangerous inside a loop.',
     predict:{q:'Adding 0.2 s of latency to the open speed chain: what happens?',opts:['The response shifts right by 0.2 s and nothing else','It overshoots, as delay in a loop does','The 10–90 % rise time grows by 0.2 s'],a:0}}]],
  q:[['Why do most small robots run speed open-loop at this level?','Because the motor controller underneath already closes a fast current and velocity loop at kilohertz rates, so from the navigation stack\'s point of view the actuator is a well-behaved first-order lag. The open chain here models that: what the navigation layer sees is the inner loop\'s response, not the motor.'],
     ['The MPC\'s speed loop and the open chain reach the reference in the same time. What did the loop buy?','Rejection: a slope, a load, a weak battery would slow the open chain and the loop would compensate; and consistency between speed and steering, since the MPC plans both together and the speed it predicts is the speed its lateral plan assumes. The step response alone does not show either; a disturbance would.'],
     ['How would you identify τ on a real vehicle?','Exactly as the first experiment: command a small speed step, log the measured speed, and fit 1 − e^(−t/τ); the 10–90 % rise time divided by 2.2 is a quick estimate. Do it at several speeds and loads; if τ varies, the MPC model should carry the slowest value and the margin analysis the fastest.']],
  check:{q:'The speed actuator is a first-order lag with τ = 0.45 s and no delay. What is the 10–90 % rise time of its step response, in seconds?',a:0.99,tol:0.02,unit:'s',how:'rise ≈ 2.2τ = 2.2 × 0.45 = 0.99 s.'}},
 {t:'The estimator in the loop',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> The analysis assumes the controller knows where the robot is. In the simulator, as on a real vehicle, it knows only the estimate, and the estimate has its own dynamics: an EKF between beacon readings integrates odometry, so most of the time it follows the true motion smoothly, and at each reading it jumps. The loop sees those jumps as disturbances and the noise as extra phase scatter. This lesson closes the gap between the control chapters and the localization ones: the estimator is part of the loop, and its errors are the loop\'s disturbances.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> The controller\'s input is <i>e</i><sub>est</sub> = <i>e</i> + (estimation error). The model\'s <i>L</i>(jω) describes the path from the true error to the command; the injection experiment measures the whole chain, estimator included, because it drives the actuator and watches what the controller does with the estimate. Where the estimator is smooth, near the crossover, the measured points still follow the model; where it is noisy, at high frequency, they scatter. A step test through the estimator shows the extra overshoot the jumps cause.</p>
<div class="eq">controller input:  e_est = e + (estimation error)
the model's L(jω) describes e → u; the injection measures the whole chain, estimator included</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> An additive estimation error at the controller input is, in the block diagram, a disturbance entering at the measurement, which the loop filters through the sensitivity function: low-frequency estimate errors are tracked (the robot follows them as if they were path offsets), high-frequency ones are attenuated by the loop\'s roll-off. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/7-where-the-model-stops">Where the model stops: chapter 6 §7</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch2/4-the-kalman-update-derived">what the EKF does between and at readings: chapter 2 §4</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The "experiments use the estimator" checkbox in the Analyze view makes the step test and the injection run through the selected localizer instead of ground truth; the model still assumes a known pose. The buttons set the EKF with odometry noise ×1, or dead reckoning with noise ×2.</p>`,
  tries:[['EKF in the loop, step test',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',loc:'ekf',odoK:1},useEst:true,test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.2},
    {setup:'Pure pursuit on the diff-drive, the EKF with beacons as the localizer, a 0.2 m step test run through the estimate.',
     see:'The model predicts 4.3 % overshoot and settling in 4.7 s; the measured true error overshoots 12.8 % and takes 6.6 s to settle within 2 %, ending about 9 % off, where the estimate happens to sit when the window closes. The response is visibly jerkier than the ground-truth version.',
     shows:'The loop is doing exactly what it should with the information it has. Every beacon update moves the estimate a few centimetres, the controller treats each move as a path error and corrects it, and the true position follows the estimate\'s jumps. The extra overshoot is not a controller problem; it is the estimator\'s noise passing through the loop\'s sensitivity function, and the cure is a better estimate or a less aggressive loop, not a different tracker.',
     predict:{q:'The step test through the EKF estimate: how will the measured true error compare with the model?',opts:['On the model: the EKF is accurate to centimetres','Lower overshoot: the estimate smooths the error','Higher overshoot, jerkier, longer to settle'],a:2}}],
   ['EKF, measure L(jω)',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',loc:'ekf',odoK:1},useEst:true,test:'inj'},
    {setup:'The injection experiment through the EKF.',
     see:'The nine measured points still follow the model near the crossover, which is why the straight-line analysis is still useful, and scatter at the upper frequencies, where the response is small and the estimate\'s jumps are a large fraction of it.',
     shows:'The model of e → u is right; the measurement includes an extra term the model does not have, and at high frequency that term wins. This is what identification on a real vehicle looks like: the model holds where the loop has gain, and the scatter above crossover is the sensor, not the plant.',
     predict:{q:'The injection through the EKF: where will the measured points scatter?',opts:['At the upper frequencies; near crossover they still fit','Everywhere equally: the noise is white','Near crossover, where the loop is most sensitive'],a:0}}],
   ['Dead reckoning in the loop',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp',loc:'dr',odoK:2},useEst:true,test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.2},
    {setup:'The same step test with dead reckoning on odometry noise ×2 as the localizer.',
     see:'The measured true error overshoots 21 % and never settles: by the end of the window it is 2.5 times the step and still drifting. The controller is tracking the path perfectly in its own estimated frame.',
     shows:'A loop closed on a drifting estimate drives the robot wherever the estimate drifts, with full confidence. Nothing in the controller can detect it, because from the controller\'s side the error is zero. This is the formal reason the localization chapters come first: the loop regulates e_est, and e_est is only as good as chapter 2 makes it.',
     predict:{q:'A step test with dead reckoning on noisy odometry: what will the true error do?',opts:['Settle with a larger overshoot, like the EKF','Overshoot, then drift without settling','Settle exactly: no beacon jumps to disturb it'],a:1}}]],
  q:[['Why does the measured overshoot go up with the estimator but the model\'s margins do not change?','The margins describe the loop from true error to command, which the estimator does not alter. The estimator adds an input, its error, at the measurement; the loop\'s response to that input is the sensitivity function, which is large near the crossover. Same loop, extra disturbance, more motion.'],
     ['Could the controller be tuned to be less sensitive to the estimate\'s jumps?','A lower crossover filters more of the high-frequency estimate noise, at the price of slower tracking; that is the usual trade on a real vehicle, where the lateral loop bandwidth is often limited by localization noise rather than by delay. The better fix is upstream: smoother estimates (a filter that spreads a beacon correction over several ticks) or a better sensor.'],
     ['With dead reckoning the true error grows while the estimated error stays zero. Which one should a safety monitor watch?','Neither is available on a real vehicle, which is the problem: the truth is unknown and the estimated error is zero by construction. A monitor watches the estimator\'s own consistency, NEES or innovation statistics, and the loop\'s commands; a loop that holds a steady steering angle on a path it believes is straight is a sign that the estimate and the world have parted.']],
  check:{q:'Pure pursuit tracks a straight path through an estimator whose lateral estimate carries a constant +3 cm bias (e_est = e + 3 cm). Once the loop has settled, how far is the true vehicle from the path, in cm?',a:3,tol:0.1,unit:'cm',how:'A constant (zero-frequency) estimate error is tracked: the loop drives e_est = e + 3 cm to zero, so e = −3 cm, 3 cm off the path.'}},
 {t:'Curves: does the straight-line analysis hold?',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Every lesson so far linearized about a straight path. Paths have curves, and on a curve the controller works around a nonzero steering command, some trackers settle beside the path rather than on it, and the car runs into its steering-rate limit. The question is whether the Bode and Nyquist plots still mean anything there, and the answer, which this lesson checks experimentally, is yes, provided the gains are read about the right operating point.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> On a circle of radius <i>R</i> the robot must hold a steady turn rate <i>v</i>₀/<i>R</i>, and the deviations about that steady state are still two integrators: θ̇<sub>e</sub> = <i>g</i>(<i>u</i> − <i>u</i>₀), ė = <i>v</i>₀θ<sub>e</sub>. So the same Bode and Nyquist plots apply, with the gains read by finite differences about the settled state on the circle instead of on the straight. Pure pursuit measures its lookahead along the path, so it follows a circle exactly and its gains barely move. Rear-wheel feedback carries the curvature as feedforward and settles on the path too. Stanley and PID have no curvature feedforward; Stanley needs a standing cross-track error to hold the steady steering angle, and the Offset chip reports where it settles. The car on a 4 m circle also runs into its steering-rate limit during the step, which the message under the experiments reports.</p>
<div class="eq">θ̇_e = g·(u − u₀),   ė = v₀·θ_e     about the settled point on the circle</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The Frenet equations with κ ≠ 0: θ̇<sub>e</sub> = ω − <i>v</i>κ cos θ<sub>e</sub>/(1 − κ<i>e</i>). At the settled point the controller\'s steady output cancels the curvature term; linearizing the remainder about that point gives the same two integrators with <i>u</i>₀ subtracted. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch5/7-curves-the-operating-point-moves">Which trackers settle where: chapter 5 §7</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/1-the-vehicle-in-path-coordinates">the operating point in the loop model: chapter 6 §1</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The Curve R 4 m and Curve R 2 m buttons in the Analyze view change the operating point to a circle. The loop is settled on the circle before the gains are read, the Offset chip reports the steady lateral offset (positive inside the turn), and the step test is measured about that offset.</p>`,
  tries:[['Pure pursuit, R = 2 m',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pp'},curveR:2,test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.2},
    {setup:'Pure pursuit on the diff-drive, operating point a 2 m circle, 0.2 m step test.',
     see:'Offset chip 0.0 cm. The gains read on the circle, K_e = 1.81 and K_θ = 1.75, are within 10 % of the straight-line 1.98 and 1.78; phase margin 63.8°, crossover 1.91 rad/s, essentially the straight-line loop. The step overshoots 5.2 % measured against 3.6 % predicted and settles in 4.6 s.',
     shows:'Pure pursuit on a circle is pure pursuit: the carrot is measured along the path, so the circle through it is the path\'s own circle and there is no standing error. The gains shift slightly because the geometry of the chord changes on a curve, and the analysis caught that by reading them where the loop actually sits.',
     predict:{q:'Pure pursuit settled on a 2 m circle: what offset and gains will the analysis read?',opts:['Zero offset, gains within about 10 % of the straight line','A standing offset inside the turn and gains halved','Zero offset, but the loop is much slower on the curve'],a:0}}],
   ['PID, R = 2 m',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{ctl:'pid'},curveR:2,test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.2},
    {setup:'PID on the bearing to the carrot (k_p = 2, k_d = 0.15, no integral), on the 2 m circle.',
     see:'Phase margin 74°, crossover 2.2 rad/s, ζ = 0.75; the step overshoots 3.0 % measured against 2.9 % predicted and settles in 4.3 s. The offset chip reads zero: the standing steering comes from a standing bearing error to the carrot, which on a circle is inside the turn, not from a lateral offset.',
     shows:'A controller with no curvature feedforward can still sit on the path if its error signal itself encodes the curve, as the bearing to a carrot measured along the path does. The straight-line analysis holds here too, once the gains are read on the circle.',
     predict:{q:'PID on the bearing, no curvature feedforward, on the circle: where will it settle?',opts:['With a standing lateral offset, like Stanley','On the path: the bearing to the carrot encodes the curve','It cannot settle without an integrator'],a:1}}],
   ['Car, Stanley, R = 4 m',{level:LV_CAR,set:{ctl:'stanley'},curveR:4,test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.2},
    {setup:'The car with Stanley on a 4 m circle.',
     see:'The offset chip reads 4.5 cm inside the turn: Stanley holds the steady steering angle with a standing front-axle error. The loop about that point has 73° of phase margin and real dominant poles, no overshoot, rise 3.2 s, settling 5.8 s measured against 5.7 s predicted; the message reports the steering-rate limit active for about half a second at the start of the step.',
     shows:'A tracker without feedforward pays for the curve in offset, and the analysis still works about that offset. The slow, overdamped response is Stanley\'s character on this car at 0.6 m/s (k_soft dominates the denominator), and the saturation note is the one piece the linear model misses: a 0.2 m step on a 4 m circle asks for a steering rate the car does not have for the first half second.',
     predict:{q:'Stanley on the 4 m circle: what will the Offset chip and the step show?',opts:['Zero offset and a quick, underdamped step','An offset outside the turn and ringing from the rate limit','A few cm of offset inside the turn and a slow, overdamped step'],a:2}}]],
  q:[['Why read the gains on the circle rather than reuse the straight-line ones?','Because the controller\'s law is nonlinear in the errors and the derivatives differ between operating points: pure pursuit\'s chord geometry, Stanley\'s arctangent, PID\'s bearing all have different local slopes on a curve. Finite differences at the settled point give the loop the controller actually closes there; the straight-line gains would be a guess that is 10 % off for pure pursuit and worse for the others.'],
     ['Which trackers would show an offset on a curve, and how big?','Any law whose steady steering must come from a standing error: Stanley (front-axle error), a pure lateral PD on e and θ_e without feedforward. The size is the steady steering angle divided by the lateral gain, δ₀/K_e in the car\'s units; rear-wheel feedback\'s feedforward and pure pursuit\'s carrot geometry supply δ₀ without an error, and an integrator would remove it slowly.'],
     ['The car saturated its steering rate on the 4 m circle. What does that imply for the planner?','That the planned curvature should leave steering authority in reserve, which is the 85 % rule in Hybrid A*: a path at the lock leaves nothing for the step, and a step is what every disturbance looks like to the tracker. Planning and control meet in the steering budget.']],
  check:{q:'The diff-drive at v₀ = 1.2 m/s settles on a circle of radius R = 3 m. What steady turn rate u₀ = v₀/R must it hold, in rad/s?',a:0.4,tol:0.01,unit:'rad/s',how:'u₀ = v₀/R = 1.2/3 = 0.4 rad/s.'}},
 {t:'When the wheels don\'t go where they point',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Every model in this lab so far assumes the car goes exactly where its front wheel points: the turn rate is <i>v</i> tan δ / <i>L</i>, and nothing else. At walking pace that is true. A real car corners because its tyres push sideways, and a tyre pushes sideways only by slipping, so at speed the wheels do not go where they point. Planners, trackers and the MPC here are all built on the kinematic model; this lesson puts a car under them that does not obey it, and shows what the mismatch costs.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Tyre slip: each tyre moves at a small angle to where it points, its slip angle, and pushes sideways in proportion, with a cornering stiffness per axle. In a steady turn the front tyres of most cars slip more than the rears, so the car turns less than the geometry says: it understeers. How much less grows with the square of the speed, through the understeer gradient. The rear slides too, so the centre of mass moves at a sideslip angle β to the heading, and the car's odometry, which works the turn rate out from the steering angle, reads a turn the car did not make.</p>
<div class="eq">α_f = δ − (v_y + l_f·r)/v_x      α_r = −(v_y − l_r·r)/v_x      F_y = C·α,  |F_y| ≤ μ·F_z
steady turn:   r/δ = v/(L + K_us·v²)      K_us = (m/L)·(l_r/C_f − l_f/C_r)      v_ch = √(L/K_us)
slippery floor (m 10 kg, C_f 40, C_r 90 N/rad, μ 0.25):  K_us = 0.0875 rad/(m/s²),  v_ch = 2.62 m/s
at 1.5 m/s the car turns at 75 % of v·tanδ/L;  at 2 m/s, 63 %</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> Newton's law sideways and the moment balance about the centre of mass, with the slip angles above and linear tyres, give a linear model in the lateral velocity <i>v</i><sub>y</sub> and the yaw rate <i>r</i>. Set its derivatives to zero and the steady yaw rate per unit of steering is the formula above; with stiff tyres (<i>C</i> → ∞) it is the kinematic <i>v</i>/<i>L</i>. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch1/4-the-dynamic-bicycle-model">The dynamic bicycle model: chapter 1 §4</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch1/5-understeer-the-characteristic-speed-and-stability">understeer and the characteristic speed: §5</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> Vehicle → <b>Car (dynamic: tyre slip)</b> runs the dynamic bicycle model under the same longitudinal chain (command, lag, acceleration and steering-rate limits): states <i>v</i><sub>y</sub> and <i>r</i> at the centre of mass, the pose still at the rear axle, tyres clipped at μ times their load. The <b>Slippery floor</b> preset makes the lab car's characteristic speed 2.6 m/s instead of a passenger car's 27, so the effects show at lab speeds; <b>Grippy</b> is kinematic to within 3 % below 2 m/s. The map's Sideslip layer draws the velocity of the centre of mass against the heading. The odometry reports <i>v</i> tan δ / <i>L</i> unless the yaw-rate gyro is on (Localize tab, Odometry). These runs lift the curve cap <i>a</i><sub>lat</sub> to 1.5 m/s² so the car takes its turns at speed.</p>`,
  tries:[['Kinematic car at 1.5 m/s',{sim:true,level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'car',vmax:2,vcruise:1.5,alat:1.5},plot:'cte',layers:{slip:true}},
    {setup:'L1 open floor, the kinematic car at 1.5 m/s with pure pursuit and Hybrid A*, curve cap 1.5 m/s².',
     see:'All 4 goals in 40.8 s, cross-track error 4.2 cm RMS (13 cm at most).',
     shows:'The reference: the car the trackers were designed for, at the same speed as the next run.',
     predict:{q:'The kinematic car at 1.5 m/s with the curve cap raised to 1.5 m/s²: how will it track?',opts:['Within a few cm RMS, all goals reached','It loses the path in the turns at this cap','It slides: 1.5 m/s² is past the grip'],a:0}}],
   ['Slippery floor, same speed',{sim:true,level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'dyn',...DYN_PRESETS.slippery,vmax:2,vcruise:1.5,alat:1.5},plot:'cte',panes:[['yawr','yawk'],['beta']],layers:{slip:true}},
    {setup:'The same run with the car with tyre slip on the slippery floor. Plots: the yaw rate <i>r</i> against the kinematic <i>v</i> tan δ / <i>L</i>, and the sideslip β.',
     see:'All 4 goals in 42.0 s, but the cross-track error is 12.1 cm RMS (37 cm at most), three times the kinematic car\'s. In every turn the yaw rate sits below <i>v</i> tan δ / <i>L</i>, on average at 0.76 of it; β reaches 12° and the arrow on the map points visibly off the heading; the steering sits at its lock 7 % of the time.',
     shows:'The tracker asks for a curvature through the kinematic model and gets three quarters of it, so it lags into every turn and steers harder to catch up. Nothing in its code knows why: the plant changed under it.',
     predict:{q:'The same run on the slippery floor: how will the yaw rate compare with v tan δ/L in the turns?',opts:['About equal: the tracker corrects the slip','Above it: the rear slides and the car over-rotates','About three quarters of it, and the error triples'],a:2}}],
   ['Dead reckoning on the sliding car',{sim:true,level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'dyn',...DYN_PRESETS.slippery,vmax:2,vcruise:1.5,alat:1.5,loc:'dr'},plot:'cte',panes:[['yawr','yawk']],layers:{slip:true,trail:true}},
    {setup:'The slippery run steered by dead reckoning: the estimate integrates the odometry, which takes the turn rate from the steering encoder, <i>v</i> tan δ / <i>L</i>. On this level the odometry has no noise and no bias.',
     see:'With the kinematic car this estimate is exact. Here it goes wrong in the first turns: in every turn the odometry reports more turning than the car did. The car reaches the first goal, then collides with a pillar, gets stuck and skips the other goals (1 of 4); at the end the estimate is 0.52 m and 6.7° off the truth.',
     shows:'Slip is an odometry error that no calibration removes: the encoders measure exactly what the wheels did, and the wheels did not move the car the way the kinematic model says. A filter that trusts this odometry is biased in every turn.',
     predict:{q:'Dead reckoning from the steering encoder on the sliding car, noise-free odometry: what will the estimate do?',opts:['Stay exact: the odometry has no noise and no bias','Report more turning than happened and drift off','Report less turning than happened'],a:1}}],
   ['Same, with a yaw-rate gyro',{sim:true,level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'dyn',...DYN_PRESETS.slippery,vmax:2,vcruise:1.5,alat:1.5,loc:'dr',gyro:true},plot:'cte',layers:{slip:true,trail:true}},
    {setup:'The same, with the yaw-rate gyro on: the odometry reports the true yaw rate.',
     see:'All 4 goals in 41.6 s. The heading estimate stays within 0.2° of the truth, but the position drifts to 0.18 m by the end.',
     shows:'A gyro fixes the heading, which is why every real car has one. The sideways slide of the rear axle, <i>v</i><sub>y</sub> − <i>l</i><sub>r</sub><i>r</i>, is in neither the encoders nor the gyro, so position still needs an outside reference: beacons, lidar against the map, GPS.',
     predict:{q:'Adding the yaw-rate gyro: what does it fix and what remains?',opts:['Heading and position both stay exact','Heading fixed; position still drifts from the sideways slide','Nothing: the gyro measures the same turn as the encoder'],a:1}}]],
  q:[['Why does the car turn less than its steering says, rather than more?','Because this car understeers: K_us > 0, which needs l_r/C_f > l_f/C_r. With the centre of mass slightly forward and softer front tyres, the fronts must slip more than the rears to carry their share of the turn, and the extra front slip is steering that does not turn the car. Swap the stiffnesses (C_f 90, C_r 40) and K_us turns negative: the car turns more than geometry says, and above a critical speed its yaw motion is unstable.'],
     ['The Grippy preset is kinematic to within 3 % below 2 m/s. When would the lab car need the dynamic model with realistic tyres?','When K_us·v² is no longer small against L: with K_us = 0.0046 rad/(m/s²) the gain falls 10 % at about 3.8 m/s, and the characteristic speed is 11.5 m/s. Small, slow robots on good floors are kinematic; road cars, racing robots and anything on a slippery surface are not.'],
     ['The tracker steers harder in a turn to make up for the slip. Why does it still lag?','Because it only reacts to the error the slip has already caused. A feedforward that knew the car understeers would add K_us·a_y of steering in advance, as production lane-keeping controllers do; the trackers here feed forward the path curvature through the kinematic L/R and nothing else.']],
  check:{q:'A car with m = 1500 kg, L = 2.7 m, l_f = 1.2 m, l_r = 1.5 m, C_f = 80 000 N/rad, C_r = 100 000 N/rad: what is its characteristic speed v_ch = √(L/K_us), in m/s?',a:26.83,tol:0.5,unit:'m/s',how:'K_us = (m/L)(l_r/C_f − l_f/C_r) = 555.6 × (1.875e-5 − 1.2e-5) = 0.00375 rad/(m/s²); v_ch = √(2.7/0.00375) = √720 = 26.8 m/s.'}},
 {t:'Running out of grip',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Every vehicle has a grip limit. The tyres can push sideways with at most μ times their load, so the car can corner at most at μ<i>g</i> of lateral acceleration; ask for a tighter turn at that speed and physics declines. Planners respect the limit through a lateral-acceleration cap on the speed profile, a number that stands for the tyres. This lesson sets the cap above what the floor can give and watches what an understeering car does when it runs out.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> The front tyres of an understeering car reach their limit first. From then on more steering adds slip angle but no force: the car follows the tightest circle the grip allows, <i>v</i>²/μ<i>g</i>, and pushes wide of the path. The tracker sees the cross-track error grow and steers harder, up to the lock, and nothing happens. Only slowing down helps, and the trackers here slow down only for the curvature the speed profile planned for, not for the error.</p>
<div class="eq">a_y = v²·κ ≤ μ·g          slippery floor: μg = 0.25 × 9.81 = 2.45 m/s²
tightest turn the grip allows at speed v:  R = v²/(μg) = 1.63 m at 2 m/s (the steering could do 0.96 m)
speed profile:  v ≤ √(a_lat/κ)   (Control tab; a_lat is the planner's model of the grip)</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> Each axle's sideways force is clipped at μ times its static load, μ<i>m g l</i><sub>r</sub>/<i>L</i> at the front and μ<i>m g l</i><sub>f</sub>/<i>L</i> at the rear, and in a steady turn each axle carries the same share of <i>m a</i><sub>y</sub>, so both reach their limit at <i>a</i><sub>y</sub> = μ<i>g</i>. Turning in also needs yaw torque from the front tyres, so they saturate a little before the car as a whole reaches μ<i>g</i>. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch1/6-the-limits-and-where-the-kinematic-model-stops">The limits: chapter 1 §6</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch5/1-path-coordinates-and-the-speed-profile">the speed profile: chapter 5 §1</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> The car with tyre slip on the slippery floor at a cruise speed of 2 m/s on L1, with three settings of the curve cap <i>a</i><sub>lat</sub>. The Control health light turns amber when <i>a</i><sub>y</sub> passes 0.8 μ<i>g</i>, the Sideslip arrow turns amber with it, and the plots show <i>a</i><sub>y</sub> against μ<i>g</i> and the cross-track error.</p>`,
  tries:[['Cap below the grip: a_lat 0.6',{sim:true,level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'dyn',...DYN_PRESETS.slippery,vmax:2.5,vcruise:2,alat:0.6},plot:'cte',panes:[['ay','mug']],layers:{slip:true,trail:true}},
    {setup:'Slippery floor, cruise 2 m/s, the lab\'s default cap of 0.6 m/s², a quarter of μg.',
     see:'All 4 goals in 39.5 s, cross-track error 5.8 cm RMS; <i>a</i><sub>y</sub> peaks at 0.94 m/s², 38 % of μg.',
     shows:'With the speed profile honest about the grip the car is slower in the turns and stays near the path, even though its yaw gain is a third below kinematic at this speed.',
     predict:{q:'Cruise 2 m/s with the cap at a quarter of μg: how will the car track?',opts:['Near the path, a few cm RMS, slower in the turns','Wide of every turn: it is above v_ch','It spins: the rear lets go first'],a:0}}],
   ['Cap at 0.8 μg: a_lat 2',{sim:true,level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'dyn',...DYN_PRESETS.slippery,vmax:2.5,vcruise:2,alat:2},plot:'cte',panes:[['ay','mug']],layers:{slip:true,trail:true}},
    {setup:'The cap raised to 2 m/s², just below the 0.8 μg at which the health light warns.',
     see:'All 4 goals in 36.1 s, faster, but the cross-track error is 18 cm RMS and 54 cm at most; <i>a</i><sub>y</sub> peaks at 1.49 m/s² (61 % of μg) because the car never quite gets round the turns it was planned through.',
     shows:'Below the limit the tyres are still linear, but they need large slip angles, and the understeer makes the car run wide of every planned turn. Speed bought with grip margin is paid for in tracking error.',
     predict:{q:'The cap at 2 m/s², just under 0.8 μg: what happens to time and error?',opts:['Faster with about the same error: the tyres are still linear','Slower: the car has to steer more','Faster, but the error triples as the car runs wide'],a:2}}],
   ['Cap above the grip: a_lat 6',{sim:true,level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'dyn',...DYN_PRESETS.slippery,vmax:2.5,vcruise:2,alat:6},plot:'cte',panes:[['ay','mug']],layers:{slip:true,trail:true}},
    {setup:'The cap at 6 m/s², more than twice what the floor can give: the speed profile no longer slows for the turns.',
     see:'The car pushes wide at the first sharp turn: off-path replan at 10.5 s, a collision with a pillar at 25.7 s, and a goal skipped after the car is stuck; 3 of 4 goals. The cross-track error reaches 1.0 m; <i>a</i><sub>y</sub> peaks at 2.13 m/s², 87 % of μg; the front tyres reach their limit (6 % of the run), the steering sits at its lock for 23 % of it, and the Control light is amber in the turns.',
     shows:'At the limit, steering is no longer a control input: the tracker can turn the wheels to the lock and the car does not turn any tighter. The only remedy is speed, and it has to come before the turn, which is the speed profile\'s job, so its a<sub>lat</sub> has to be the grip the floor really has.',
     predict:{q:'The cap above the grip, 6 m/s²: what will the car do at the first sharp turn?',opts:['Steer to the lock and push wide, off the path','Spin and stop','Follow the path: the tracker steers harder to compensate'],a:0}}],
   ['Kinematic car, same cap',{sim:true,level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'car',vmax:2.5,vcruise:2,alat:6},plot:'cte',layers:{trail:true}},
    {setup:'The kinematic car with the cap at 6 m/s².',
     see:'All 4 goals in 34.6 s, cross-track error 5.7 cm RMS.',
     shows:'The kinematic car has no grip limit, so nothing in the simulation would ever tell a planner tuned on it that 6 m/s² is impossible. That is why the cap exists, and why it is a number about the tyres, not about the controller.',
     predict:{q:'The kinematic car with the impossible 6 m/s² cap: how will it do?',opts:['It pushes wide like the dynamic car','It tracks fine: it has no grip limit','It saturates its steering rate and rings'],a:1}}]],
  q:[['Why does the understeering car push wide rather than spin?','Its front tyres reach their limit first: they carry a larger share of the slip, so they saturate while the rears still have grip. The front then slides and the car turns less (pushes wide); the rear holds, so the yaw motion stays stable. An oversteering car saturates the rear first, the tail steps out, and the yaw motion can diverge, which is why road cars are tuned to understeer.'],
     ['The tracker steered to the lock and gained nothing. What would have helped?','Braking before the turn, so that v²κ fits under μg, and in the turn easing off the steering to the angle at which the front tyres give their most force. The trackers here do neither, because their model has no grip limit; a speed profile with the real μg does the first. The MPC with the tyre model (Control → Tyre model in the prediction) knows the limit and stops asking for steering that earns nothing, but it holds the reference speed, so the braking still has to come from the speed profile (<a class="lsref" href="#lesson=ctl:17">The moose test</a>).'],
     ['The health light warns at 0.8 μg. Why not at μg?','Because the lateral acceleration the car reaches is a lagging signal: by the time it reads μg the front tyres are already saturated. A margin of 20 % leaves room for the yaw torque of turning in, for a gust of disturbance, and for a μ that is lower than assumed, the last of which is the usual cause on real roads.']],
  check:{q:'On a road with μ = 0.6 (g = 9.81 m/s²) a car travels at 20 m/s. What is the tightest turn radius the grip allows, R = v²/(μg), in metres?',a:67.96,tol:1.4,unit:'m',how:'μg = 0.6 × 9.81 = 5.886 m/s²; R = 20²/5.886 = 400/5.886 = 68.0 m.'}},
 {t:'Gain scheduling breaks above v_ch',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> The gain-scheduling lesson (<a class="lsref" href="#lesson=ctl:10">Speed changes the plant</a>) showed pure pursuit's lookahead schedule keeping the loop the same at every speed. That works because the kinematic plant gain is <i>v</i>/<i>L</i>, known exactly. With tyres the plant gain is <i>v</i>/(<i>L</i> + <i>K</i><sub>us</sub><i>v</i>²): it rises, peaks at the characteristic speed, and falls, and the car's own yaw mode adds a lag that the kinematic model does not have. A schedule designed for the kinematic plant mis-schedules above <i>v</i><sub>ch</sub>, and a tracker tuned on it can lose its margin entirely.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> Two things change with the tyres. The plant gain falls below <i>v</i>/<i>L</i> by the factor <i>L</i>/(<i>L</i> + <i>K</i><sub>us</sub><i>v</i>²), which lowers the crossover; and the yaw mode, two poles of the car's own, costs phase near the crossover, more so as it slows and loses damping with speed. Pure pursuit's damping, ζ ≈ 0.71 at every speed on the kinematic car, is no longer fixed. A tracker with a high crossover, like rear-wheel feedback with its default gains, can have its phase margin eaten whole.</p>
<div class="eq">kinematic:  θ̇ₑ = (v/L)·δ                 with tyres:  r/δ = G_r(s),   G_r(0) = v/(L + K_us·v²)
yaw mode on the slippery floor:  1 m/s: −9.2, −17.9 1/s     2 m/s: ω_n 7.5 rad/s, ζ 0.90     4 m/s: ω_n 5.5, ζ 0.62
G_r(0) ÷ (v/L):  0.87 at 1 m/s,  0.63 at 2,  0.43 at 3,  0.30 at 4</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The steady gain is chapter 1 §5's formula; the yaw mode is the eigenvalues of the 2 × 2 model of §4, overdamped at low speed and underdamped above about 1.5 m/s on this floor. The Analyze view puts that model between the steering and the heading: θ̇ₑ = <i>r</i>, and the lateral error grows at <i>v</i>θₑ plus the rear axle's sideways slide. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch1/5-understeer-the-characteristic-speed-and-stability">Chapter 1 §5</a> · what the extra poles do to a loop: <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch6/3-the-open-loop-l-s-with-the-clock-in-it">chapter 6 §3</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> These buttons open Analyze on L1 with the car. With the car with tyre slip selected, the analysis uses the linear tyre model at the operating speed: the vehicle paragraph gives the yaw gain against <i>v</i>/<i>L</i>, the transfer function shows <i>G</i><sub>r</sub>(<i>s</i>), and the step test runs the full nonlinear car. The schedule is pure pursuit's own, <i>L</i><sub>d</sub> = 0.5 + 0.5·<i>v</i>.</p>`,
  tries:[['Kinematic car, scheduled PP, 4 m/s',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'car',ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.5,ppK:0.5,vmax:4,vcruise:4}},
    {setup:'The kinematic car at 4 m/s with pure pursuit and its speed schedule (L_d = 2.5 m).',
     see:'Crossover 3.52 rad/s, phase margin 60.5°, ζ 0.71, overshoot 4.3 %. At 1 m/s the same schedule gives 2.20 rad/s, 62.4° and the same 4.3 %.',
     shows:'On the kinematic plant the schedule works: four times the speed, the same loop.',
     predict:{q:'Scheduled pure pursuit on the kinematic car at 4 m/s versus 1 m/s: what changes?',opts:['Crossover rises fourfold with the speed','Roughly the same loop: ζ 0.71, about 60° of margin','Damping falls as the lookahead grows'],a:1}}],
   ['Slippery floor, scheduled PP, 1 m/s',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'dyn',...DYN_PRESETS.slippery,ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.5,ppK:0.5,vmax:4,vcruise:1}},
    {setup:'The car with tyre slip on the slippery floor at 1 m/s, below v_ch.',
     see:'The yaw gain is 87 % of v/L; crossover 1.86 rad/s, phase margin 46°, ζ 0.59, overshoot 9.9 %. The open-loop poles of the yaw mode sit at −9.2 and −17.9 1/s.',
     shows:'Even below the characteristic speed the tyres cost 16° of phase margin: the yaw mode is only a decade above the crossover.',
     predict:{q:'The slippery car at 1 m/s, below v_ch: will the tyres cost margin?',opts:['No: below v_ch the car is kinematic','Yes, the loop goes unstable even here','About 15° of phase margin, the yaw mode only a decade above crossover'],a:2}}],
   ['Slippery floor, scheduled PP, 4 m/s',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'dyn',...DYN_PRESETS.slippery,ctl:'pp',ppL0:0.5,ppK:0.5,vmax:4,vcruise:4}},
    {setup:'The same schedule at 4 m/s, above v_ch = 2.62 m/s.',
     see:'The yaw gain is 30 % of v/L; crossover 1.33 rad/s, lower than at 1 m/s; phase margin 27°, M_s 2.1, ζ 0.25, overshoot 46 %, settling 11.6 s.',
     shows:'The schedule assumed a plant gain rising with v and got one falling, plus a yaw mode at 5.5 rad/s with ζ 0.62 right above the crossover. The loop is slower and much less damped: the opposite of what the schedule was for.',
     predict:{q:'The same schedule at 4 m/s, above v_ch: what happens to crossover and damping?',opts:['Crossover rises and ζ holds 0.71','Crossover rises but ζ falls below 0.5','Crossover falls below its 1 m/s value and ζ drops to about 0.25'],a:2}}],
   ['Rear-wheel feedback at 2 m/s',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'dyn',...DYN_PRESETS.slippery,ctl:'rwf',vmax:4,vcruise:2},test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.05},
    {setup:'Rear-wheel feedback with its default gains (k_e 6, k_θ 3) on the slippery car at 2 m/s, and a 5 cm step test in the simulator.',
     see:'Phase margin 0.3°, M_s 102, ζ 0.003: on the edge of instability. The step overshoots 87 % predicted and 85 % measured and rings for 16 s. The kinematic car with the same gains at the same speed has 50° and 9.9 %.',
     shows:'Rear-wheel feedback\'s high crossover (6.9 rad/s on the kinematic car) puts it right on the yaw mode, whose lag takes the whole margin. A controller tuned on the kinematic model is not slightly worse on the real car; it can be unusable.',
     predict:{q:'Rear-wheel feedback with its default gains on the slippery car at 2 m/s: how much margin is left?',opts:['Roughly the kinematic 50°','About 25°: underdamped but usable','Almost none: on the edge of instability'],a:2}}],
   ['Re-tuned for the tyres: k_e 1.5, k_θ 2.5',{level:LV_DIFF,set:{veh:'dyn',...DYN_PRESETS.slippery,ctl:'rwf',rwKe:1.5,rwKth:2.5,vmax:4,vcruise:2},test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.05},
    {setup:'The same car and speed with a softer lateral gain and more heading damping, found by dragging the dominant pole in the pole plot.',
     see:'Phase margin 40°, crossover 2.9 rad/s, ζ 0.65; the step overshoots 5.7 % predicted and 5.6 % measured and settles in 2.6 s.',
     shows:'The fix is a lower crossover, below the yaw mode, and more damping, designed on the model with the tyres in it. That is gain scheduling done properly: gains tabulated against speed, each checked on the dynamic model at that speed.',
     predict:{q:'Softer k_e = 1.5 and k_θ = 2.5 on the same car: what will the step show?',opts:['A few per cent overshoot, settling in a few seconds','Still ringing: lower gains cannot fix a plant problem','Overdamped and very slow, tens of seconds'],a:0}}]],
  q:[['Why does the crossover fall with speed above v_ch, when on the kinematic car it rises?','Pure pursuit\'s gains fall as 1/v (K_e = 2v/L_d², with L_d growing as v), and on the kinematic car the plant gain v/L rises as v, so the loop gain levels off. With tyres the plant gain v/(L + K_us v²) falls as 1/v above v_ch, so the product falls as 1/v² and the crossover with it.'],
     ['How would you schedule rear-wheel feedback for this car?','Pick the closed-loop poles you want at each speed, below the yaw mode, and compute the gains that place them on the dynamic model at that speed (the Analyze what-if does exactly this when you drag a pole). Tabulate them against speed and check each breakpoint for margin. Dividing by v and v² as on the kinematic car keeps the poles where they were, which is now too close to the yaw mode.'],
     ['Would MPC fix this?','Not with its default prediction model, the kinematic car: it schedules itself on the wrong plant just as pure pursuit does. With Control → Tyre model in the prediction ticked, the MPC predicts with the dynamic bicycle model, and its gains, now also on v_y and r, come from the plant it is really driving: on the moose test at 3.5 m/s the dominant poles go from ζ 0.22 to 0.70 (<a class="lsref" href="#lesson=ctl:17">The moose test: an MPC that knows the tyres</a>). That is what vehicle-dynamics MPC in production cars does.']],
  check:{q:'A car with L = 2.7 m and K_us = 0.00375 rad/(m/s²) at v = 30 m/s: what is its steady yaw gain G_r(0) = v/(L + K_us·v²) as a fraction of the kinematic v/L?',a:0.444,tol:0.01,unit:'',how:'L/(L + K_us v²) = 2.7/(2.7 + 0.00375 × 900) = 2.7/6.075 = 0.444.'}},
 {t:'The moose test: an MPC that knows the tyres',b:`<p><b>Why this matters.</b> Collision avoidance at speed is where the vehicle model stops being a detail. In the moose test (ISO 3888-2) a car at speed swerves round an obstacle into the other lane and back. A planner can draw the swerve; whether the car follows it is decided by the tyres. A controller that predicts with the kinematic model believes every steering angle is a turn it gets at once, and on the slippery floor above the characteristic speed that belief is off by a factor of three.</p>
<p><b>The idea.</b> An MPC is only as good as its prediction model. Put the dynamic bicycle model in it and the prediction knows two things the kinematic one does not: the car turns at <i>v</i>/(<i>L</i> + <i>K</i><sub>us</sub><i>v</i>²) per unit of steering, a third of <i>v</i>/<i>L</i> here, and the yaw rate builds up through the car's own yaw mode instead of appearing with the steering angle. So the optimizer starts the swerve earlier and steers further for the same path. It also knows the tyre forces stop at μ<i>F</i><sub>z</sub>, so steering past the grip earns nothing in its prediction. The kinematic MPC learns all of this only through feedback, after the error has appeared, and at 3.5 m/s there is not enough road for that.</p>
<div class="eq">x = [x, y, θ, v, δ, v_y, r]     u = [a, δ̇]     (the kinematic MPC: x = [x, y, θ, v, δ])
v̇_y = (F_f·cos δ + F_r)/m − v·r     ṙ = (l_f·F_f·cos δ − l_r·F_r)/I_z     F = clamp(C·α, ±μ·F_z)
slippery floor at 3.5 m/s:  r/δ = v/(L + K_us·v²) = 0.36·v/L     yaw mode −3.9 ± 4.2j 1/s
5 substeps per 0.1 s prediction step (linearly implicit Euler); with stiff tyres it is the kinematic model</div>
<p><b>Where it comes from.</b> The lateral equations are chapter 1's dynamic bicycle model, the one the simulator runs; the MPC integrates them inside each prediction step and moves the rear axle with the resulting velocities, so its Jacobians, sensitivities and constraints work unchanged on the larger state. <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch7/10-the-tyre-model-in-the-prediction">The tyre model in the prediction: chapter 7 §10</a> · <a class="chref" href="#notes=ch1/4-the-dynamic-bicycle-model">the dynamic bicycle model: chapter 1 §4</a></p>
<p><b>What the lab does.</b> L7, the moose test: a 3.2 m road between barriers, a box in the right lane at 8 m and one in the left lane at 15 m. The car with tyre slip on the slippery floor (<i>v</i><sub>ch</sub> 2.6 m/s) enters at 3.5 m/s; the curve cap <i>a</i><sub>lat</sub> is 8 m/s², so the reference holds the speed through the slalom. Hybrid A* plans the slalom once on the known map and the MPC tracks it with obstacle constraints from the lidar. Control → <b>Tyre model in the prediction</b> switches the MPC's model; it reads <i>v</i><sub>y</sub> and <i>r</i> from the simulator, as a gyro and a sideslip estimator would give them. The Analyze view reads the MPC's two extra gains, on <i>v</i><sub>y</sub> and <i>r</i>, and puts them in the loop.</p>`,
  tries:[['Kinematic MPC at 3.5 m/s',{sim:true,level:LV_MOOSE,panes:[['yawr','yawk'],['cte']],layers:{slip:true}},
    {setup:'L7 as it loads: the MPC with its kinematic prediction model. Plots: the yaw rate <i>r</i> against <i>v</i> tan δ / <i>L</i>, and the cross-track error.',
     see:'The MPC starts the swerve at 1.35 s and steers up to 15°. The yaw rate reaches about a third of <i>v</i> tan δ / <i>L</i> (0.51 against 1.41 rad/s at 1.8 s); the car slides 0.65 m right of its path and hits the first box at 2.4 s. It stays there: this level does not reverse.',
     shows:'The kinematic MPC is not a bad controller of this car; it is a good controller of a different car. Its feedback sees the error grow and steers harder, but the car answers late and at a third of the expected rate, and the box is two seconds away.',
     predict:{q:'The kinematic MPC entering the moose test at 3.5 m/s on the slippery floor: what happens?',opts:['It passes, with a large but recoverable error','It hits the first box: the car turns at a third of the expected rate','It spins out between the boxes'],a:1}}],
   ['Tyre model in the MPC',{sim:true,level:LV_MOOSE,set:{mpcDyn:true},panes:[['yawr','yawk'],['cte']],layers:{slip:true}},
    {setup:'The same run with Control → Tyre model in the prediction ticked.',
     see:'Steering starts at 0.8 s, 0.55 s earlier, and goes up to 21°. The yaw rate still averages 41 % of <i>v</i> tan δ / <i>L</i> in the turns, but the car passes both boxes and reaches the goal in 10.5 s: cross-track error 9.9 cm RMS and 0.40 m at worst, sideslip up to 3.3°, <i>a</i><sub>y</sub> up to 92 % of μ<i>g</i>. Each solve takes about 0.2 ms longer.',
     shows:'The tyres did not change; the prediction did. Knowing that the car turns late and weakly, the optimizer begins earlier and asks the wheels for more, and the steering it plans is the one that gets the body round the box. With stiff tyres the two models drive the same line to within a centimetre, so nothing else about the controller changed.',
     predict:{q:'With the tyre model in the prediction, how does the MPC change its steering?',opts:['Starts earlier and steers further; the car gets through','Steers less, trusting the tyres; the car gets through','Same steering; the car still hits the box'],a:0}}],
   ['Analyze: the kinematic MPC on this car',{level:LV_MOOSE,test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.05},
    {setup:'The lateral loop at 3.5 m/s with the kinematic MPC, and a 5 cm offset step in the simulator.',
     see:'Phase margin 69°, <i>M</i><sub>s</sub> 1.24: by its margins a comfortable loop. But the dominant closed-loop poles sit at 1.5 rad/s with ζ 0.22: the step overshoots 50 % (50.1 % in the simulator) and takes 11.5 s to settle.',
     shows:'The margins are read where |<i>L</i>| crosses 1 (7.5 rad/s here) and the sensitivity peak; the slow, lightly damped pair that the tyres add shows in neither, only in the poles and the step. Read the poles as well as the margins when the plant has dynamics the controller was not designed for.',
     predict:{q:'The kinematic MPC\'s lateral loop at 3.5 m/s on this car: what will the margins and the poles say?',opts:['Low margins and low damping, both agreeing','Comfortable margins, but a lightly damped slow pair and 50 % overshoot','Comfortable margins and ζ ≈ 0.7'],a:1}}],
   ['Analyze: with the tyre model',{level:LV_MOOSE,set:{mpcDyn:true},test:'step',stepKind:'offset',stepAmp:0.05},
    {setup:'The same with the tyre model in the prediction.',
     see:'Phase margin 50°, <i>M</i><sub>s</sub> 1.30, crossover 6.6 rad/s; the dominant poles have ζ 0.70, and the step overshoots 4.1 % (4.1 % in the simulator) and settles in 2.7 s. The kinematic MPC on the kinematic car at the same speed: ζ 0.73, 3.2 %, 2.0 s.',
     shows:'With the tyres in its model the MPC gets back the loop it has on the kinematic car, without a change to a single weight. Its two extra gains, on <i>v</i><sub>y</sub> and <i>r</i>, are yaw-rate and sideslip feedback that the optimizer derived from the model; a hand-built controller would need them designed.',
     predict:{q:'With the tyre model, what happens to the dominant poles and the step?',opts:['ζ back to about 0.7, overshoot a few per cent','Margins improve but the step still overshoots 50 %','The loop slows to stay under the yaw mode; settling in 10 s'],a:0}}],
   ['Grippy tyres at 4 m/s',{sim:true,level:LV_MOOSE,set:{Cf:500,Cr:700,mu:0.9,vcruise:4},panes:[['yawr','yawk'],['cte']],layers:{slip:true}},
    {setup:'L7 with the grippy tyres (<i>v</i><sub>ch</sub> 11.5 m/s) at 4 m/s, the kinematic MPC.',
     see:'It passes in 10.0 s, 0.24 m off its path at worst; in the turns the yaw rate is 89 % of <i>v</i> tan δ / <i>L</i>.',
     shows:'Realistic tyres keep a small car nearly kinematic at lab speeds. The tyre model matters where <i>K</i><sub>us</sub><i>v</i>² is not small against <i>L</i>: high speed, soft tyres, low grip. That is why it is standard in road-vehicle MPC and rare in warehouse robots.',
     predict:{q:'Grippy tyres (v_ch 11.5 m/s) at 4 m/s with the kinematic MPC: how will it do?',opts:['Hits a box: 4 m/s is faster than the failed run','Passes: the yaw rate is near 90 % of kinematic','Passes only because it slows down'],a:1}}]],
  q:[['The tyre model got through without braking. Would it brake for a swerve it cannot make?','Not in this lab. At μ 0.1 it hits the first box at 3.5 m/s, as the kinematic MPC does. Its cost holds the speed to the reference, and with the front tyres saturated, slowing down pays off in its prediction only late in the two-second horizon, through a lateral error the Gauss–Newton step does not trade for a speed error. The speed has to come from upstream: a reference speed that respects the grip, v ≤ √(μg/κ), which is the curve cap a_lat set to the real μg.'],
     ['The MPC reads v_y and r from the simulator. What would a real car use?','The yaw rate from a gyro, which every car with stability control has. The lateral velocity is not measured directly: it is estimated from the gyro, a lateral accelerometer and the wheel speeds through a model like this one (a sideslip observer), or from a dual-antenna GNSS. Its error enters the MPC as model error, so production controllers often lean on r and weight the sideslip state loosely.'],
     ['Hybrid A* plans the slalom with arcs down to 1.13 m. Is that plan feasible at 3.5 m/s?','No tyre could follow those arcs at that speed: 3.5²/1.13 = 10.8 m/s², four times μg here. The MPC survives because it does not follow the plan exactly; it cuts the arcs within the obstacle constraints. A planner for speed needs a curvature limit from the grip, κ ≤ μg/v², or a search over the dynamic model; the MPC can only make the best of the plan it is given.']],
  check:{q:'The slippery-floor car (L = 0.6 m, K_us = 0.0875 rad/(m/s²)) at 3.5 m/s holds a steady steering angle of 10°. What yaw rate r = δ·v/(L + K_us·v²) results, in rad/s?',a:0.365,tol:0.01,unit:'rad/s',how:'L + K_us v² = 0.6 + 0.0875 × 12.25 = 1.672 m; r/δ = 3.5/1.672 = 2.093 1/s; δ = 10° = 0.1745 rad; r = 2.093 × 0.1745 = 0.365 rad/s.'}},
];
